const modules=[{"title": "Foundations: explain the problem before the algorithm", "file": "01_Foundations.md", "text": "\n# Foundations: explain the problem before the algorithm\n\n> [!abstract] Study focus\n> Explain returns, values and the Markov property before discussing an algorithm. Connect every definition to MEDS.\n\n[[OralDefence/README|Study guide]] \u00b7 [[07_Sources_and_Presentation_Checklist|Course source links]]\n\n## 1. What is reinforcement learning?\n\nAn agent learns how to act through interaction, aiming to maximise expected cumulative reward. An action can change both the immediate reward and the future situations the agent encounters. This makes delayed consequences and exploration central.\n\n**Say aloud:** \u201cIn MEDS, the agent controls the delivery vehicle. It receives observations and rewards from the simulator, and must learn a sequence of navigation, pickup, parking and delivery actions.\u201d\n\nUnlike supervised learning, the training signal is not a labelled correct action for every observation. The environment generates experience. Unlike a simple bandit, actions affect later states: picking up supplies changes which hospital should be visited next.\n\n**Course:** W1 pp. 11\u201319; W2 pp. 4\u20135, 24\u201328.\n\n## 2. MDP, state and observation\n\nAn MDP is described by $(\\mathcal S,\\mathcal A,P,R,\\gamma)$: states, actions, transition dynamics, rewards and a discount factor. A policy $\\pi(a\\mid s)$ gives the probability of action $a$ in state $s$.\n\n| MDP symbol | What it represents in MEDS |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\mathcal S$ | Possible complete situations: vehicle, task progress and other relevant environment state |\n| $\\mathcal A$ | Available actions, such as movement, Pickup and Deliver |\n| $P$ | Rules/probabilities for what happens after an action |\n| $R$ | Reward rule for the resulting events |\n| $\\gamma$ | Weight placed on later rewards |\n| $\\pi(a\\mid s)$ | Probability of choosing action a given state s |\n\nThink of an MDP as a description of the decision problem, not the learning algorithm that solves it. State is the information needed to predict what happens next; observation is what the agent actually receives.\n\nThe controlled Markov property means the next-state/reward distribution depends on the current state and action, without needing the earlier history. **Memoryless does not mean independent of the current state.**\n\nAn **environment state** contains what determines the environment\u2019s behaviour. An **observation** is the information exposed to the agent. If different hidden states produce the same observation but different future outcomes, that observation alone may not be Markov. A POMDP explicitly allows partial observation.\n\n**MEDS connection:** the 17-component observation includes position and task progress, but not the entire visited-cell history or complete traffic state. Because rewards and traffic updates depend on internal history/state, do not claim \u201c17 inputs prove full observability.\u201d This is a project-specific concern inferred from the implementation, not an experimentally quantified effect on performance.\n\n**Course:** W2 pp. 15\u201323; W3 pp. 3\u20138, 23\u201328. **Project:** P2, P5 in the source map.\n\n## 3. Reward, return and discount\n\nUse the course convention: action $A_t$ is followed by reward $R_{t+1}$.\n\n$$G_t=R_{t+1}+\\gamma R_{t+2}+\\cdots+\\gamma^{T-t-1}R_T.$$\n\n### Read the return equation\n\n| Symbol | Plain meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| $t$ | Time of the action we are evaluating |\n| $G_t$ | Total discounted reward still ahead at time t |\n| $R_{t+1}$ | First reward, received after the current action |\n| $R_{t+2}$ | Reward one step further into the future |\n| $\\gamma$ | Discount: how much future rewards count |\n| $\\cdots$ | Continue adding the intermediate rewards |\n| $T$ | Terminal time; $R_T$ is the last reward |\n| $\\gamma^{T-t-1}$ | Weight of the last reward, based on how far away it is |\n\n**Say aloud:** \u201cAdd the rewards from this action onward, reducing the weight of later rewards.\u201d\n\nFor rewards 2, \u22121 and 5 with discount 0.9, start at the end: the last return is 5; the previous return is \u22121 + 0.9 \u00d7 5 = 3.5; the starting return is 2 + 0.9 \u00d7 3.5 = 5.15. In the shorter recurrence below, $G_{t+1}$ means the remaining return starting one step later, and $G_T=0$ means there is no future reward beyond terminal.\n\nEquivalently, $G_t=R_{t+1}+\\gamma G_{t+1}$, with $G_T=0$ at a true terminal state.\n\n- **Reward:** one feedback signal.\n- **Return:** discounted rewards over the future trajectory.\n- **Discount $\\gamma$:** relative weight of later rewards. With $\\gamma=0$, only the next reward matters. Near one, distant rewards retain more influence.\n- **Learning rate $\\alpha$:** update size. It is not a discount factor.\n\nA finite episode can use $\\gamma=1$. Infinite continuing sums need conditions for finiteness; $\\gamma<1$ with bounded rewards is a standard sufficient condition.\n\n**MEDS connection:** a delivery reward may arrive long after navigation and pickup. A smaller discount weakens that distant signal. A larger discount does not itself solve exploration.\n\n**Course:** W3 pp. 14\u201321; local textbook \u00a7\u00a73.3\u20133.4.\n\n## 4. State value, action value and advantage\n\n$$V^\\pi(s)=\\mathbb E_\\pi[G_t\\mid S_t=s],\\qquad\nQ^\\pi(s,a)=\\mathbb E_\\pi[G_t\\mid S_t=s,A_t=a].$$\n\n### Read the value definitions\n\n| Symbol | Plain meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| $V^\\pi(s)$ | Average future return from state s when following policy \u03c0 |\n| $Q^\\pi(s,a)$ | Average return after choosing action a in state s, then following \u03c0 |\n| Superscript $\\pi$ | \u201cUnder this policy\u201d; it is a label, not a power |\n| $\\mathbb E_\\pi$ | Average over possible trajectories generated under the policy |\n| $G_t$ | Return from this point onward |\n| $\\mid$ | \u201cGiven that\u201d; restrict the average to the stated condition |\n| $S_t=s$ | We are in the particular state s |\n| $A_t=a$ | The first action is the particular action a |\n\n**Say aloud:** \u201cV averages what happens from a state. Q averages what happens after a specified first action.\u201d\n\nImagine repeatedly starting at the same clinic. V includes whichever actions the policy chooses there. Q for Pickup fixes the first action to Pickup, then lets the policy make later decisions. One successful trip is a return sample; it is not the true expected value.\n\n$V$ asks how good a state is under a policy. $Q$ asks how good a particular first action is, followed by that policy. A sampled return is one outcome; a value is an expectation over possible outcomes.\n\n$$V^\\pi(s)=\\sum_a\\pi(a\\mid s)Q^\\pi(s,a),\\qquad\nA^\\pi(s,a)=Q^\\pi(s,a)-V^\\pi(s).$$\n\n### Read the policy average and advantage\n\n$\\sum_a$ means add over all available actions. $\\pi(a\\mid s)$ is the probability of each action; multiply it by that action\u2019s Q-value before adding. $A^\\pi(s,a)$ is **advantage**: how much that action\u2019s expected return exceeds the policy\u2019s usual return at this state. Here A names a value function; in $A_t$, A names the selected action.\n\nFor two actions with Q-values 4 and 8 and probabilities 0.25 and 0.75, V is 0.25 \u00d7 4 + 0.75 \u00d7 8 = 7. Their advantages are 4 \u2212 7 = \u22123 and 8 \u2212 7 = +1. The second action is better than the policy average, even though the first still has a positive Q-value.\n\nThe average is **policy-weighted**, not necessarily uniform. Positive advantage means better than the policy\u2019s average at that state, not necessarily positive absolute return.\n\n**MEDS connection:** at a clinic, Pickup could have higher value than another movement even if both have negative expected returns, because Pickup may be less bad and enable later progress.\n\n**Course:** W4 pp. 4\u20137; W5 pp. 6\u201318; W10 p. 35; textbook \u00a73.5.\n\n## 5. Bellman expectation versus optimality\n\nThe Bellman expectation equation splits the expected return into one reward and the discounted value of the successor:\n\n$$V^\\pi(s)=\\sum_a\\pi(a\\mid s)\\sum_{s',r}p(s',r\\mid s,a)\n[r+\\gamma V^\\pi(s')].$$\n\n### Read the Bellman expectation equation from the inside out\n\n| Piece | Role |\n| --- | --- |\n| $s$, $a$ | Current state and a candidate action |\n| $s'$ | Next state; the prime means \u201csuccessor,\u201d not a derivative |\n| $r$ | Possible immediate reward |\n| $p(s',r\\mid s,a)$ | Probability of that next-state/reward outcome after choosing a in s |\n| $r+\\gamma V^\\pi(s')$ | Immediate reward plus discounted continuation value |\n| $\\sum_{s',r}$ | Average over all possible outcomes using their probabilities |\n| $\\pi(a\\mid s)$ | Weight the action by how often the policy selects it |\n| $\\sum_a$ | Average across the policy\u2019s possible actions |\n| $V^\\pi(s)$ | The resulting expected return at the current state |\n\n**Recipe:** evaluate each outcome \u2192 average outcomes for each action \u2192 average actions under the policy. For one deterministic action giving reward 2 and reaching a state worth 5, with discount 0.9, its expected return is 2 + 0.9 \u00d7 5 = 6.5.\n\nFor an optimal action value:\n\n$$Q^*(s,a)=\\sum_{s',r}p(s',r\\mid s,a)\n[r+\\gamma\\max_{a'}Q^*(s',a')].$$\n\n### What changes in the optimality equation?\n\n$Q^*$ means the optimal action value; the star is a label for optimality, not multiplication. $a'$ is a candidate action at the next state. $\\max_{a'}$ selects the largest next-action value. The transition probability $p$ and sum over $s',r$ still average random outcomes.\n\n**Say aloud:** \u201cTake this action now, then choose the best available action afterwards.\u201d If the next-action values are 4 and 8, the continuation uses 8. With immediate reward 2 and discount 0.9, that branch contributes 2 + 0.9 \u00d7 8 = 9.2. The agent can choose an action, but cannot choose which random transition occurs.\n\n**What changed?** Expectation follows the specified policy. Optimality chooses the best continuation. Do not replace every policy-weighted average by a maximum when evaluating a fixed stochastic policy.\n\n**Visual question strategy:** identify whether the diagram branches over actions, transitions, or both. Weight action branches by $\\pi$ for policy evaluation; weight random transitions by their probabilities; use a maximum only at an optimal choice.\n\n**Course:** W4 pp. 20\u201332; W5 pp. 7\u201319. Practise [[05_Worked_Calculations|calculations 2 and 3]].\n\n## 6. Model-free does not mean \u201cno simulator\u201d\n\nModel-free methods learn values or policies from experience without using an explicit transition/reward model for planning. Model-based methods use a given or learned dynamics model to reason about consequences.\n\nThe MEDS simulator has transition rules, but REINFORCE, PPO and A2C can still be model-free: their learners use sampled interactions rather than querying a full transition model to plan actions. A separate scripted planning diagnostic does not turn the training algorithm into model-based RL.\n\n**Course:** W6 pp. 8\u20139; W11 pp. 4\u201314.\n\n---\n\n[[OralDefence/README|Previous]] \u00b7 [[OralDefence/README|Study map]] \u00b7 [[02_Learning_Methods|Next]]\n"}, {"title": "Learning methods: understand what the target contains", "file": "02_Learning_Methods.md", "text": "\n# Learning methods: understand what the target contains\n\n> [!abstract] Study focus\n> Identify the learning target: a complete return, a bootstrapped estimate, or an expectation under a model.\n\n[[OralDefence/README|Study guide]] \u00b7 [[07_Sources_and_Presentation_Checklist|Course source links]]\n\n## Prediction, control and dynamic programming\n\nPrediction estimates the value of a given policy. Control improves behaviour to obtain a better policy. Policy iteration alternates policy evaluation and greedy improvement. Value iteration combines an optimality backup with successive value updates, then extracts a policy.\n\nClassical tabular DP uses the transition/reward model to average over possible outcomes. It is useful when that model and a manageable state space are available. MEDS uses sampled neural learning; that is a design choice, not proof that planning is impossible.\n\n**Course:** W4 pp. 34\u201339; W5 pp. 20\u201326, 30\u201342.\n\n## Monte Carlo and temporal difference\n\nMonte Carlo prediction waits for the episode return:\n\n$$V(S_t)\\leftarrow V(S_t)+\\alpha[G_t-V(S_t)].$$\n\n| Symbol / piece | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| $V(S_t)$ on the right | Current estimate for the visited state |\n| $\\leftarrow$ | Replace the stored value with the result on the right |\n| $G_t$ | Complete observed return: the target |\n| $G_t-V(S_t)$ | Prediction error: target minus current estimate |\n| $\\alpha$ | Fraction of that error used for this update |\n\n**Say aloud:** \u201cMove the old estimate partway toward what actually happened.\u201d If V is 4, the return is 10 and the learning rate is 0.1, the error is 6 and the new value is 4 + 0.1 \u00d7 6 = 4.6. This is value prediction; REINFORCE instead uses returns to update policy parameters.\n\nTD(0) uses a one-step target:\n\n$$\\delta_t=R_{t+1}+\\gamma V(S_{t+1})-V(S_t),\\qquad\nV(S_t)\\leftarrow V(S_t)+\\alpha\\delta_t.$$\n\n| Symbol / piece | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\delta_t$ | TD error: new target minus old estimate |\n| $R_{t+1}$ | Immediate observed reward |\n| $\\gamma$ | Discount on the estimated continuation |\n| $V(S_{t+1})$ | Current value estimate at the next state |\n| $R_{t+1}+\\gamma V(S_{t+1})$ | One-step TD target |\n| $-V(S_t)$ | Subtract the estimate we want to correct |\n| $\\alpha\\delta_t$ | The correction applied to that estimate |\n\n**Say aloud:** \u201cUse one real reward and an estimate of the rest.\u201d With reward 2, next V of 6 and discount 0.9, the target is 7.4. If current V is 4, the error is 3.4; learning rate 0.1 produces V = 4.34.\n\n**Bias versus variance, simply:** bias is systematic error in the target\u2019s average; variance is how much targets fluctuate across samples. A wrong successor estimate can bias TD. A long random trajectory can make MC returns fluctuate widely.\n\n**Bootstrapping** means using a current estimate inside the target for another estimate. Sampling and bootstrapping are different: MC samples without bootstrapping; TD samples and bootstraps; DP can bootstrap while averaging over the model.\n\nFor a fixed policy, a complete sampled return is an unbiased but potentially high-variance value target. A TD target can be biased by an inaccurate successor value, but often has lower variance and allows earlier updates. These are target-level intuitions, not a guarantee that every MC experiment loses to every TD experiment.\n\nFirst-visit MC uses the first occurrence of each state in an episode; every-visit MC uses each occurrence. Neither should be confused with updating after each single transition before an episode finishes.\n\n**MEDS connection:** collisions can end episodes before a delivery reward is experienced. REINFORCE cannot learn from a delivery reward it never observes. TD can propagate estimated future value earlier, but an inaccurate critic and poor exploration remain problems.\n\n**Course:** W6 pp. 14\u201325, 30, 44; W7 pp. 8\u201316, 26; textbook \u00a7\u00a75.1, 6.1\u20136.2.\n\n## On-policy and off-policy\n\nOn-policy learning evaluates or improves the policy generating its experience. Off-policy learning can learn about a target policy different from the behaviour policy collecting the data.\n\nSARSA uses the next action actually selected by the behaviour policy:\n\n$$Q(S_t,A_t)\\leftarrow Q(S_t,A_t)+\\alpha[\nR_{t+1}+\\gamma Q(S_{t+1},A_{t+1})-Q(S_t,A_t)].$$\n\n### Decode SARSA\n\n$S_t$ is the current state, $A_t$ the action taken, $R_{t+1}$ the next reward, $S_{t+1}$ the next state, and $A_{t+1}$ the action actually selected there. These five quantities give SARSA its name. $Q(S_t,A_t)$ is the value being corrected; $\\alpha$ is the learning rate and $\\gamma$ discounts the continuation.\n\nThe square brackets contain **target minus old estimate**. The target uses the value of the chosen next action, even if that action was an exploratory choice. If reward is 2, discount 0.9 and the chosen next action is worth 5, the target is 6.5.\n\nQ-learning uses the greedy continuation target:\n\n$$Q(S_t,A_t)\\leftarrow Q(S_t,A_t)+\\alpha[\nR_{t+1}+\\gamma\\max_a Q(S_{t+1},a)-Q(S_t,A_t)].$$\n\n### Decode Q-learning\n\nThe symbols match SARSA except for $\\max_a$: consider every candidate action at the next state and take the largest estimated Q-value. The $a$ inside this maximum is a temporary action index, not necessarily the action actually taken next.\n\n**Say aloud:** \u201cCorrect this action\u2019s value toward the reward plus the best estimated continuation.\u201d If successor Q-values are 8, 11 and 5, the target with reward 2 and discount 0.9 is 11.9. With old Q of 5 and learning rate 0.1, the new Q is 5.69. SARSA using the chosen value 5 would instead update to 5.15.\n\n**Say aloud:** \u201cSARSA learns the value of the policy it follows, including exploration. Q-learning can behave exploratorily while targeting a greedy policy.\u201d\n\nFor a terminal successor, the future term is zero. At an artificial time limit, whether to bootstrap depends on the task definition and algorithm; do not automatically equate every truncation with a true terminal state. The reported MEDS REINFORCE implementation uses observed rewards through truncation with no bootstrap.\n\n**What happens to $G_t$ in Q-learning?** The definition of return does not change. The update replaces the complete sampled-return target with $R_{t+1}+\\gamma\\max_aQ(S_{t+1},a)$. This directly addresses the anecdotal example question.\n\n**Course:** W6 p. 33; W7 pp. 29\u201346; textbook \u00a7\u00a76.4\u20136.5. Truncation statement: project P3/P5.\n\n## Function approximation and DQN\n\nA table has a separate entry per state or state\u2013action pair. A function approximator shares parameters across inputs and can generalise to combinations not individually stored. Approximation error and unstable learning are possible.\n\nDQN approximates $Q(s,a)$ with a neural network. Replay samples stored transitions to reduce temporal correlation and reuse experience. A target network supplies a more slowly changing bootstrap target, helping stabilise learning:\n\n$$y=R_{t+1}+\\gamma\\max_aQ_{\\mathrm{target}}(S_{t+1},a).$$\n\n### Decode the DQN target\n\n$y$ is the target number the online Q-network learns to predict. $R_{t+1}$ is the observed reward; $\\gamma$ is the discount; $S_{t+1}$ is the next state; $a$ ranges over its actions; $\\max_a$ chooses the largest value. $Q_{\\mathrm{target}}$ is a separate, more slowly updated copy of the value network.\n\n**Simple analogy:** when correcting an estimate, it is easier to learn if the reference answer is not changing as quickly as the estimate itself. The target network slows that change; replay supplies less temporally correlated training examples. Neither guarantees stable or successful learning.\n\nAt a true terminal successor, omit the continuation term so y is just the immediate reward.\n\nA CNN is useful for images. MEDS REINFORCE uses a numerical feature vector and an MLP; the rendered Pygame display is not its policy input.\n\n**Course:** W8 pp. 4\u201314, 25\u201338; W9 pp. 38\u201345. Project input: P2/P3.\n\n## Comparison to rehearse\n\n| Method | Learning signal | Model used for planning? | Bootstrap? | Main oral-defence distinction |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Tabular DP | Expected model backup | Yes | Yes | Requires model-based expectations |\n| MC prediction | Complete sampled return | No | No | Waits for episode outcome |\n| TD(0) | Reward + successor value | No | Yes | Updates before the episode ends |\n| SARSA | Reward + value of chosen next action | No | Yes | On-policy control |\n| Q-learning / DQN | Reward + maximum next Q | No | Yes | Off-policy greedy target |\n| REINFORCE | Return-weighted policy gradient | No | No | Direct stochastic policy learning |\n| A2C / PPO | Advantage-based policy update with value learning | No | Typically yes | Actor\u2013critic; PPO limits update incentives through clipping |\n\nThe last row describes standard implementations; verify an actual run\u2019s rollout, critic and advantage settings before claiming a particular configuration.\n\n---\n\n[[01_Foundations|Previous]] \u00b7 [[OralDefence/README|Study map]] \u00b7 [[03_Policy_Gradients|Next]]\n"}, {"title": "REINFORCE, actor\u2013critic and PPO", "file": "03_Policy_Gradients.md", "text": "\n# REINFORCE, actor\u2013critic and PPO\n\n> [!abstract] Study focus\n> REINFORCE uses sampled returns; actor\u2013critic uses value estimates; PPO changes the policy objective through clipping.\n\n[[OralDefence/README|Study guide]] \u00b7 [[07_Sources_and_Presentation_Checklist|Course source links]]\n\n> [!tip] Want the equations explained step by step?\n> Read [[08_REINFORCE_Explained|REINFORCE explained]] for every symbol, a complete worked loss calculation, and connections to the MEDS code.\n\n## Policy gradients\n\nA parameterised policy $\\pi_\\theta(a\\mid s)$ maps inputs to action probabilities. In an episodic task, one objective is $J(\\theta)=\\mathbb E[G_0]$. Gradient ascent increases that objective; a software optimiser performing gradient descent minimises its negative surrogate loss.\n\n**Decode the objective:** in $J(\\theta)=\\mathbb E[G_0]$, J is the expected starting return, \u03b8 is the network\u2019s weights and biases, the expectation symbol means an average over possible episodes, and $G_0$ is the discounted return from step zero. A good single episode does not establish a high expected return.\n\nThe score term $\\nabla_\\theta\\log\\pi_\\theta(a\\mid s)$ tells us how parameters affect the sampled action\u2019s log probability. Weighting it by return or advantage assigns greater reinforcement to better outcomes.\n\n**Course:** W10 pp. 13\u201324; textbook \u00a7\u00a713.1\u201313.3.\n\n## REINFORCE: the complete explanation\n\n1. Sample a complete episode using the current policy.\n2. Compute reward-to-go backwards: $G_t=R_{t+1}+\\gamma G_{t+1}$.\n3. Increase log probabilities of actions weighted by their returns, optionally subtracting an action-independent baseline.\n4. Update the policy, then collect fresh experience.\n\nFor the discounted episodic formulation in the course pseudocode:\n\n$$\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta+\\alpha\\gamma^t(G_t-b)\\nabla_\\theta\\log\\pi_\\theta(A_t\\mid S_t).$$\n\n### Read the update one piece at a time\n\n| Symbol | Plain meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\theta$ | Policy network weights and biases |\n| $\\leftarrow$ | Replace the old parameters with the updated parameters |\n| $\\alpha$ | Learning rate: size of the adjustment |\n| $\\gamma^t$ | Discount the action time relative to episode start |\n| $G_t$ | Complete discounted reward-to-go after this action |\n| $b$ | Action-independent comparison baseline, in the same units as G |\n| $G_t-b$ | Whether the sampled return was better or worse than the reference |\n| $\\pi_\\theta(A_t\\mid S_t)$ | Probability of the sampled action given the state |\n| $\\log$ | Natural logarithm of that probability |\n| $\\nabla_\\theta$ | Sensitivity of the log probability to each policy parameter |\n\n**Say aloud:** \u201cMove the weights toward increasing an action\u2019s probability when its return is better than the baseline, and in the opposite direction when it is worse.\u201d This describes the individual gradient contribution; combined network updates can interact.\n\nIn this generic equation there is no return divisor. The implemented MEDS equation below uses scaled returns and a correspondingly scaled baseline.\n\nThe outer $\\gamma^t$ weights time relative to the start-state objective; the discount inside $G_t$ weights rewards relative to the action time. Course overview slides sometimes abbreviate this weighting. State which formulation you are discussing.\n\n**Why a baseline?** Subtracting an action-independent baseline leaves the expected score contribution zero:\n\n$$\\sum_a\\pi_\\theta(a\\mid s)b(s)\\nabla_\\theta\\log\\pi_\\theta(a\\mid s)\n=b(s)\\nabla_\\theta\\sum_a\\pi_\\theta(a\\mid s)=0.$$\n\n### Why the baseline cancels on average\n\n$\\sum_a$ adds over possible actions. Each action is weighted by its probability $\\pi_\\theta(a\\mid s)$. The baseline $b(s)$ may depend on the state but is the same for all actions at that state. Treat it as fixed while differentiating the policy.\n\nUsing the log-derivative identity, probability times its log gradient becomes the gradient of the probability. All action probabilities sum to one, and the gradient of the constant one is zero. Thus the baseline contributes zero **on average**, although it changes individual sampled updates.\n\nFor the full symbol explanation and numerical baseline examples, see [[08_REINFORCE_Explained#6. Baseline: ask \u201cbetter than what?\u201d|the baseline walkthrough]].\n\nAn appropriate baseline can reduce variance; an arbitrary baseline is not guaranteed to do so. A negative return can still produce a positive update if it is better than the baseline. A baseline is not a reward added to the environment.\n\n**Course:** W10 pp. 22\u201324, 28; textbook \u00a7\u00a713.3\u201313.4. This algebra is a study derivation of the baseline property.\n\n## Our implemented REINFORCE loss\n\nThe reported MEDS run uses a scalar exponential-moving-average baseline, return divisor 100, and an entropy bonus:\n\n$$\\mathcal L=-\\frac1B\\sum_{i=1}^B\\sum_{t=0}^{T_i-1}\\gamma^t\n\\left[\\log\\pi_\\theta(a_{i,t}\\mid o_{i,t})(G_{i,t}/100-b)\n+0.01\\,H(\\pi_\\theta(\\cdot\\mid o_{i,t}))\\right].$$\n\n### Decode the batch loss\n\n| Piece | Role |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\mathcal L$ | Batch loss the optimiser minimises |\n| Leading minus | Converts maximisation of the surrogate into minimisation |\n| $B$, $1/B$ | Number of episodes and averaging across them |\n| $i$, $t$, $T_i$ | Episode index, step index, and length of episode i |\n| $\\sum_i\\sum_t$ | Add all step contributions within all episodes |\n| $\\gamma^t$ | Discount this step\u2019s policy and entropy contributions |\n| $a_{i,t}$, $o_{i,t}$ | Sampled action and numerical observation |\n| $\\theta$, $\\pi_\\theta$ | Network parameters and the resulting action policy |\n| $\\log\\pi_\\theta$ | Log probability of the selected action |\n| $G_{i,t}/100-b$ | Scaled return above the earlier scalar baseline |\n| $H$ | Entropy: spread of probabilities across the actions |\n| $\\cdot$ inside $\\pi_\\theta(\\cdot\\mid o)$ | All possible actions, rather than one selected action |\n| $0.01$ | Strength of the entropy bonus |\n\n**Say aloud:** \u201cSum return-weighted action log probabilities and entropy bonuses, discount them, average episode sums, then negate the result.\u201d See [[08_REINFORCE_Explained#9. The full MEDS loss, now assembled|a complete numerical loss calculation]].\n\nHere $o$ explicitly denotes the supplied observation. The notebook calls its reward after action $a_t$ `rewards[t]`; this corresponds to course notation $R_{t+1}$, not a different return definition.\n\n- The policy consumes 17 inputs through two 128-unit Tanh layers and produces eight logits. A categorical distribution converts logits into action probabilities.\n- The batch contains up to eight complete episodes. Losses sum over timesteps and average across episodes.\n- Returns and baselines are detached from the policy gradient.\n- Adam uses learning rate 0.001; gradient norm is clipped to 1.0.\n- After the policy update, $b\\leftarrow0.9b+0.1\\overline{G/100}$, starting at zero. The current batch uses the earlier baseline.\n- Entropy $H=-\\sum_a\\pi(a\\mid o)\\log\\pi(a\\mid o)$ encourages retaining action diversity. It does not guarantee useful exploration or visits to all states.\n- Return scaling changes the relative strength of the return signal and entropy term; it is not automatically harmless to learning dynamics.\n\n**Decode the remaining implementation formulas:** in the EMA update, the arrow means replacement; the old baseline b receives weight 0.9 and the mean scaled return (the overbar denotes a mean) receives weight 0.1. In the entropy formula, the sum runs over all actions, each probability is multiplied by its natural logarithm, and the minus sign makes the result nonnegative. Here entropy measures action diversity, not task success.\n\n**Code navigation:** in the archived notebook, find `ReinforcePolicy`, `reward_to_go`, `policy_loss_terms`, `fit_baseline`, `collect_episode`, `train_reinforce` and `evaluate_checkpoint`. Explain data flow rather than memorising cell numbers.\n\n**Project sources:** P3, P5 and P6. These details describe the reported run, not every available tuning option.\n\n## Actor\u2013critic and A2C\n\nThe actor learns the policy. The critic estimates a value used to guide the actor. A one-step state-value critic produces:\n\n$$\\delta_t=R_{t+1}+\\gamma V_w(S_{t+1})-V_w(S_t).$$\n\n### Decode the critic\u2019s TD error\n\n$\\delta_t$ is the error between the new target and the old value estimate. $R_{t+1}$ is the immediate reward. $\\gamma V_w(S_{t+1})$ is the discounted predicted continuation. $V_w(S_t)$ is the current prediction being corrected. The subscript $w$ names the critic\u2019s weights; the actor\u2019s weights are called $\\theta$.\n\nWith reward 2, discount 0.9, next-state value 6 and current value 4, the error is 2 + 0.9 \u00d7 6 \u2212 4 = 3.4. This says the transition looked better than predicted. The actor can use that signal to reinforce the chosen action; the critic can use it to improve its prediction. An inaccurate critic can give an inaccurate learning signal.\n\nThis TD error can estimate advantage. The actor updates using $\\nabla\\log\\pi\\,\\delta_t$, while the critic learns to reduce value prediction error. An $n$-step target instead sums several observed rewards and bootstraps at the end of the rollout:\n\n$$\\hat G_t^{(n)}=\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\gamma^kR_{t+k+1}+\\gamma^nV_w(S_{t+n}).$$\n\n### Decode an n-step target\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\hat G_t^{(n)}$ | Estimated return using n observed steps; the hat means estimate |\n| $n$ | Number of rewards included before bootstrapping |\n| $k$ | Counter from zero to n\u22121 |\n| $\\gamma^kR_{t+k+1}$ | One observed reward, discounted by its distance from now |\n| $\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}$ | Add those n discounted rewards |\n| $\\gamma^nV_w(S_{t+n})$ | Critic\u2019s estimate of the remainder after the n steps |\n\n**Say aloud:** \u201cUse several real rewards, then estimate whatever remains.\u201d With two rewards 1 and 2, discount 0.9 and successor value 4, the target is 1 + 0.9 \u00d7 2 + 0.81 \u00d7 4 = 6.04. If the second transition truly ends the episode, there is no remainder and the target is 2.8.\n\nAt a true terminal state the bootstrap contribution is zero. A rollout length is not necessarily an episode length.\n\n**A2C** means Advantage Actor\u2013Critic; it uses advantage estimates to update the policy with synchronised rollout updates. The course\u2019s core actor\u2013critic equations explain the mechanism; the MEDS deck identifies its implementation as Stable-Baselines3 A2C with `n_steps=512`.\n\n**REINFORCE versus actor\u2013critic:** REINFORCE uses complete sampled returns and does not bootstrap its policy target. A bootstrapping critic offers earlier, often lower-variance learning signals, with possible approximation bias. Adding a Monte Carlo-trained baseline to REINFORCE does not by itself make its target bootstrapped. Our selected scalar EMA baseline is not a learned state-value critic.\n\n**Course:** W10 pp. 26\u201335; W7 pp. 14\u201316; textbook \u00a7\u00a77.1, 13.4\u201313.5. A2C implementation context: W10 actor\u2013critic lab and project P1/P7.\n\n## PPO: explain the equation, not only the acronym\n\nPPO means Proximal Policy Optimization. Define the probability ratio using the action sampled during collection:\n\n$$\\rho_t(\\theta)=\\frac{\\pi_\\theta(A_t\\mid S_t)}{\\pi_{\\mathrm{old}}(A_t\\mid S_t)}.$$\n\n### Decode the probability ratio\n\n$\\rho_t$ is the ratio for this sampled action; it is not a reward. The numerator is the action probability under the policy currently being optimised. The denominator is its probability under the frozen old policy that collected the rollout. $A_t$ and $S_t$ identify the same action and state in both probabilities; $\\theta$ is the current actor\u2019s parameter vector.\n\nIf an action\u2019s probability rises from 0.2 to 0.3, the ratio is 1.5: it is now 50% more probable. A ratio of 1 means unchanged, and 0.5 means half as probable. A ratio can exceed one even though a probability cannot.\n\nThe clipped objective is maximised:\n\n$$L^{\\mathrm{CLIP}}=\\mathbb E_t\\left[\n\\min\\left(\\rho_t\\hat A_t,\n\\operatorname{clip}(\\rho_t,1-\\epsilon,1+\\epsilon)\\hat A_t\\right)\\right].$$\n\n### Decode the clipped objective\n\n| Symbol / operation | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| $L^{\\mathrm{CLIP}}$ | Clipped surrogate objective to maximise; CLIP is a label |\n| $\\mathbb E_t$ | Empirical average across sampled rollout timesteps |\n| $\\rho_t$ | New-to-old probability ratio for the sampled action |\n| $\\hat A_t$ | Estimated advantage; positive means better than the state\u2019s reference value |\n| $\\epsilon$ | Allowed clipping margin around one, not an exploration probability |\n| $1-\\epsilon$, $1+\\epsilon$ | Lower and upper clipping bounds |\n| $\\operatorname{clip}$ | Restrict its argument to those bounds in this branch of the expression |\n| $\\min$ | Choose the smaller of the original and clipped advantage-weighted terms |\n\n**Work it through:** ratio 1.5, advantage +2, margin 0.2. The original term is 3; the clipped term is 1.2 \u00d7 2 = 2.4; take 2.4. With advantage \u22122, the terms are \u22123 and \u22122.4; take \u22123. Multiplying by a negative advantage changes which term is smaller.\n\nThe objective is not simply \u201calways use the clipped ratio.\u201d The minimum discourages excessively large beneficial changes while retaining penalties for changes in the wrong direction.\n\nI use $\\rho$ rather than the slide\u2019s $r$ to avoid confusing the probability ratio with reward. $\\hat A_t$ is an estimated advantage, $\\epsilon$ is the clipping parameter, and the old policy is the snapshot that collected the rollout.\n\nFor positive advantage, increasing an action\u2019s probability is beneficial, but the objective stops rewarding increases beyond $1+\\epsilon$. For negative advantage, it stops rewarding decreases below $1-\\epsilon$. The minimum selects the pessimistic surrogate term.\n\n**Important precision:** clipping is part of the objective. It does not forcibly constrain every actual probability ratio or guarantee monotonic policy improvement. See the negative-advantage calculation before saying \u201cthe ratio is always clipped.\u201d\n\nPPO typically reuses a recent on-policy rollout for several minibatch optimisation passes, then collects fresh data. This is different from unrestricted replay of old transitions in DQN. A value loss and entropy term may accompany the clipped policy objective.\n\n**Course:** W10 pp. 39\u201351, especially the visually checked equations on pp. 46\u201347; W10 PPO labs for rollout implementation context.\n\n## Thirty-second comparison\n\n\u201cREINFORCE updates a stochastic policy using complete sampled returns. Actor\u2013critic adds a value estimator, typically using bootstrapping to provide advantage signals before waiting for a complete return. PPO is commonly implemented as actor\u2013critic and changes the actor objective to discourage excessively large policy changes. None of these mechanisms guarantees that our delivery task will be solved.\u201d\n\n---\n\n[[02_Learning_Methods|Previous]] \u00b7 [[OralDefence/README|Study map]] \u00b7 [[04_Project_Defence|Next]]\n"}, {"title": "Defending MEDS: connect each claim to evidence", "file": "04_Project_Defence.md", "text": "\n# Defending MEDS: connect each claim to evidence\n\n> [!abstract] Study focus\n> Defend the observed outcomes and evaluation conditions. Reward differences do not establish delivery success or an algorithm ranking.\n\n[[OralDefence/README|Study guide]] \u00b7 [[07_Sources_and_Presentation_Checklist|Source links and presentation checklist]]\n\n## The project in 45 seconds\n\n\u201cMEDS is a simulated medical-supply delivery task on a 15 \u00d7 15 grid. The agent must collect supplies from clinics, navigate to their matching hospitals, park and deliver. Full success additionally requires all three deliveries, returning to the start and selecting Stop. We explored REINFORCE, A2C and PPO. The results show limited intermediate progress, but do not establish reliable completion of the full task. Our evaluations also differ in conditions, so they do not establish a fair algorithm ranking.\u201d\n\nThis describes a simulation study. It does not demonstrate real-world medical deployment, safety, or reduced delivery times in practice. The presentation\u2019s real-world motivation is separate from what the experiments establish.\n\n## Environment and task interface\n\n| Component | Explain aloud |\n| --- | --- |\n| Agent | A delivery vehicle controlled by a learned policy |\n| Environment | Fixed 15 \u00d7 15 grid, clinic\u2013hospital pairs, fences and optional moving traffic |\n| Actions | Eight discrete choices: Up, Down, Left, Right, Stop, Pickup, Park, Deliver |\n| Observation | 17 numerical components in the reported REINFORCE run; not camera pixels |\n| Reward | Step costs, event rewards, exploration-related shaping and penalties |\n| Success | All three deliveries, return to (0,0), then Stop |\n| Episode limit | 500 steps for the reported REINFORCE evaluation |\n| Failure ending | Traffic collision; time limit is recorded separately as truncation |\n| Experience | Generated by interaction, not a fixed labelled dataset |\n\n`reset()` starts an episode and returns observation/info. `step(action)` produces observation, reward, termination, truncation and info. `render()` makes the scene visible; it is not the learned policy\u2019s input in this run.\n\n**Observation groups:** vehicle coordinates (2); supply possession and pickup-clinic identifier (2); three completion flags (3); delivery count and parking flag (2); target clinic/hospital coordinates (4); directional traffic indicators (4). Total: 17.\n\n**Slide corrections:** clinic identifiers are 0, 1, 2; 3 is the no-current-pickup sentinel, not a fourth clinic or a count of remaining clinics. The completion set is updated at delivery in the inspected environment. Check the exact frozen environment for each algorithm rather than assuming all notebooks are identical.\n\n**Evidence:** P1 pp. 3\u20136; P2; P5. The root environment file can differ from the run\u2019s archived implementation, so the saved run identifier governs claims about that experiment.\n\n## Rewards and the task objective\n\nThe deck lists a base step penalty of \u22120.05, pickup bonuses 50/75/100, delivery priorities 60/80/120, successful return-and-Stop bonus 200, fence penalty \u22125 and traffic-collision penalty \u221225. It also lists intermediate shaping, such as visiting new cells or approaching clinics.\n\nUse these as **presentation-level reward components**, not guaranteed totals returned by every step. Several components can apply in one transition; branch conditions and environment versions matter. The deck\u2019s \u201c+60 delivery\u201d and priority-dependent delivery list need reconciliation with the specific `step()` implementation before presenting an exact reward table.\n\n**Why shape rewards?** Intermediate feedback can help with delayed credit assignment. **What can go wrong?** The agent may receive reward without completing the intended sequence. Reward improvement is therefore insufficient; measure pickups, deliveries and full success separately. This is a plausible mechanism, not proof that shaping alone caused the observed failure.\n\n**Theory:** W1 pp. 13\u201315, W3 pp. 17\u201319, W10 pp. 22\u201328. **Project:** P1 p. 5, P2/P5.\n\nFor a step-by-step explanation of the algorithm behind these settings, see [[08_REINFORCE_Explained|REINFORCE explained]].\n\n## REINFORCE settings and evidence to memorise\n\n| Setting | Reported value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Run | `20261026T025626_518818Z_combined_seed42` |\n| Checkpoint | `models/model_final.pt` |\n| Actual training steps | 4,000,054 (4,000,000 requested; completed the current episode) |\n| Training seed | 42 |\n| Policy | 17 \u2192 128 \u2192 128 \u2192 8, Tanh hidden activations |\n| Learning rate / discount | 0.001 / 0.99 |\n| Batch / return scaling | Up to 8 complete episodes / divide returns by 100 |\n| Baseline / entropy coefficient | Scalar EMA, decay 0.9 / 0.01 |\n| Evaluation | 20 episodes per mode, seeds 200000\u2013200019 |\n| Modes | Sampled categorical actions and greedy argmax actions |\n| Environment | Traffic enabled; 500-step limit; fixed map and traffic setup |\n\nCheckpoint SHA256: `ac047caccefe9c43921c413ecb3e015c0603d0c401fffd29f84dc6f88c7da12d`.\n\nThe configuration includes settings for an optional learned baseline, but `baseline_mode='ema'` selects the scalar baseline. Do not describe inactive configuration options as components of the reported run.\n\n**Evidence:** P3, P5, P6; saved configuration and evaluation JSON directly checked.\n\n## Read the configuration in plain language\n\n| Setting | What it changes | What it does not establish |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Learning rate 0.001 | Size scale used by Adam when adjusting the policy | How likely the policy is to explore |\n| Discount 0.99 | Weight retained for each additional reward delay | A 99% chance of succeeding |\n| Batch up to eight episodes | How much fresh episode experience is collected before an update | Eight steps per episode |\n| Return divisor 100 | Magnitude of return feedback compared with the other loss terms | Reward values shown in the environment itself |\n| Baseline decay 0.9 | Retain 90% of the old scalar reference, add 10% new batch information | A learned value estimate for each state |\n| Entropy coefficient 0.01 | Strength of encouragement for a broader action distribution | Guaranteed coverage of useful states |\n| Gradient norm limit 1.0 | Cap on the raw gradient vector\u2019s norm | PPO probability-ratio clipping |\n| Training seed 42 | Initialises relevant random-number generation | A particular skill level or proof of reproducibility on every platform |\n\nA **step** is one environment interaction; an **episode** is a sequence ending at termination or truncation; an **update** is one optimisation operation using the collected batch. These counts are different. The 17 \u2192 128 \u2192 128 \u2192 8 notation describes network widths: input features, two hidden layers, then eight action logits. It is not a route on the map.\n\n## How to interpret the metrics\n\nFor a defined evaluation set, the fraction of episodes with at least one pickup is:\n\n$$f_{\\mathrm{pickup}}=\\frac{N_{\\mathrm{pickup}}}{N_{\\mathrm{episodes}}}.$$\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| $f_{\\mathrm{pickup}}$ | Fraction of episodes containing at least one pickup |\n| $N_{\\mathrm{pickup}}$ | Number of episodes with a pickup, not the total pickup-event count |\n| $N_{\\mathrm{episodes}}$ | Total number of evaluation episodes |\n| Division | Convert the count into a fraction; multiply by 100 to express a percentage |\n\nTwo pickup episodes out of twenty gives 0.1, or 10%. An episode with three pickups still counts as just one pickup episode for this metric.\n\nMean deliveries is a different quantity:\n\n$$\\bar D=\\frac1N\\sum_{i=1}^{N}D_i.$$\n\nHere $\\bar D$ is mean deliveries per episode; the bar means average. N is the episode count, i indexes episodes, $D_i$ is the delivery count in episode i, and the summation adds those counts before division. If two episodes have delivery counts 0 and 2, the mean is 1 delivery per episode, while only half the episodes had any delivery. A count mean is not automatically a success percentage.\n\n**Simple reading rule:** ask \u201cwhat was counted, over how many episodes, using which checkpoint and settings?\u201d A mean collision count above one can be valid when multiple collision events are counted; it is not a probability. Full success has its own event definition: all three deliveries plus return and Stop.\n\nThese equations explain the project\u2019s metric definitions; the examples are hypothetical. They introduce no new evaluation results. Sources: P4/P6 and the comparison qualifications in P7/P8.\n\n## What the results actually show\n\n| REINFORCE final evaluation | Sampled | Greedy |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Episodes | 20 | 20 |\n| Episodes with pickups | 0/20 | 0/20 |\n| Mean deliveries | 0 | 0 |\n| Full successes | 0/20 | 0/20 |\n| Mean reward | \u221237.47 | \u221225.05 |\n| Mean length | 53.55 | 45.00 |\n| Mean invalid actions | 7.55 | 0 |\n| Traffic-collision endings | 20/20 | 20/20 |\n\n**Interpretation:** greedy evaluation earned a less negative reward and avoided invalid actions, but did not progress through the delivery task. Its recorded action counts contain only movement. Neither mode solved the task.\n\nTraining recorded 53 pickup events across 67,051 episodes, with zero deliveries and full successes. That does not contradict zero final evaluation pickups: training explores using changing policies, whereas evaluation tests a fixed checkpoint.\n\nThe separate 20M/40M-step tuning study found pickup acquisition for some configurations, but zero deliveries and full successes. The 4M run is not a matched comparison with those longer runs. At the requested 20M boundary, only the combined learning-rate/batch change recorded pickups in all evaluation episodes; this does not isolate which component caused that result.\n\nA conditional diagnostic supplied legal scripted movement and pickup before handing over to the final policy. There were zero deliveries across 120 episodes. It suggests failure persisted after pickup under those conditions; it does not measure normal-reset success and does not identify a unique causal explanation.\n\n**Evidence:** P4/P6.\n\n## How to compare the group\u2019s algorithms\n\nThe final report records PPO at 4,001,792 steps with pickups in 20/20 **no-traffic** sampled evaluation episodes, no deliveries and no full successes; episodes hit the 500-step limit. A2C reports zero success and small mean pickup/delivery counts, but the evaluation denominator and checkpoint provenance are insufficient for conversion into comparable episode percentages.\n\nThe A2C deck lists learning rate 0.0001, $\\gamma=0.995$, entropy coefficient 0.1, `n_steps=512`, `MlpPolicy`, and a 5,000,000-step target. Treat these as reported settings; they do not establish an independently verified final evaluation checkpoint.\n\n**Say aloud:** \u201cPPO showed pickup behaviour under its reported no-traffic conditions; REINFORCE did not in its final traffic-enabled evaluation. The conditions differ, so I cannot conclude that the algorithm alone explains the difference.\u201d\n\nA defensible future comparison would freeze the same environment/reward/observation definition, match training-step budgets, identify checkpoint hashes, specify action-selection modes, and use multiple training seeds and evaluation settings. Report pickup episodes, delivery counts and full successes, plus uncertainty. Keep selection seeds separate from held-out evaluation. These are proposed evaluation improvements, not completed experiments.\n\n**Evidence:** P7 \u00a7\u00a73.3.1\u20133.3.4; P8. A2C means must not be turned into episode rates without a justified denominator and definition.\n\n## Likely challenges and honest answers\n\n**\u201cWhy did it fail?\u201d** \u201cWe observed zero final task progress, frequent collision termination and movement-only greedy behaviour. Rare rewarding task sequences, partial observation and credit assignment are plausible contributors. These observations do not isolate a single cause; controlled interventions would be needed.\u201d\n\n**\u201cIs your observation Markov?\u201d** \u201cNot established. Visited-cell history affects rewards but is not fully exposed, and directional flags do not encode complete traffic dynamics. The 17-feature observation may alias distinct internal states.\u201d\n\n**\u201cDoes a different reset seed test a new map?\u201d** \u201cNo. This setup fixes map and traffic. Repeated seeds are not evidence of generalisation to new layouts.\u201d\n\n**\u201cDoes entropy prove exploration?\u201d** \u201cEntropy measures the spread of action probabilities. It does not measure coverage of states or successful task sequences. Loss sign alone does not prove the agent stopped exploring.\u201d\n\n**\u201cWhat would you change next?\u201d** \u201cFirst verify task solvability and observation consistency, then compare a single controlled change at matched budgets. Candidate changes include richer traffic/history information or targeted exploration support. These are proposals, not demonstrated improvements.\u201d\n\n**\u201cWhat did you personally implement?\u201d** Name the parts you actually wrote or validated, then walk through the functions and an artifact. This guide cannot determine authorship from file presence alone.\n\n---\n\n[[03_Policy_Gradients|Previous]] \u00b7 [[OralDefence/README|Study map]] \u00b7 [[05_Worked_Calculations|Next]]\n"}, {"title": "Worked calculations and visual-question practice", "file": "05_Worked_Calculations.md", "text": "\n# Worked calculations and visual-question practice\n\n> [!abstract] Study focus\n> Try each calculation before expanding its answer. Explain each symbol and intermediate step aloud.\n\n[[OralDefence/README|Study guide]] \u00b7 [[07_Sources_and_Presentation_Checklist|Course sources]]\n\nThese are newly constructed practice examples using the supplied course equations. They are not MEDS evaluation results. Try each question before opening its answer.\n\n## 1. Discounted return\n\n**Decode first:** $G_t$ is the return starting at action time t; $\\gamma$ is the discount per future step. The three rewards arrive after actions 0, 1 and 2. **Method:** start with the final reward, then repeatedly add the current reward to the discounted return you already calculated. **Check:** at discount zero, only the immediate reward remains.\n\nRewards after three actions are 2, \u22121, 5; $\\gamma=0.9$. Compute all returns.\n\n> [!question]- Show answer\n> $G_2=5$; $G_1=-1+0.9(5)=3.5$; $G_0=2+0.9(3.5)=5.15$.\n>\n> At $\\gamma=0$, $G_0=2$. At $\\gamma=1$, $G_0=6$. Return is not the arithmetic mean of rewards.\n\n**Course:** W3 pp. 17\u201321; W6 p. 15.\n\n## 2. Value of a short chain\n\n**Decode first:** A and B are state names here, not action and baseline symbols. The labelled arrow means \u201cmove to the next state and receive this reward.\u201d $V(A)$ and $V(B)$ are expected returns from those states. **Method:** assign terminal value zero and work backwards. **Check:** count each transition reward only once.\n\nA fixed policy takes $A\\xrightarrow{r=2}B\\xrightarrow{r=5}\\mathrm{terminal}$ with $\\gamma=0.9$. Find $V(A)$ and $V(B)$.\n\n> [!question]- Show answer\n> $V(\\mathrm{terminal})=0$, $V(B)=5$, $V(A)=2+0.9(5)=6.5$.\n>\n> The reward of 5 is paid on the transition into terminal; it is not added again as terminal-state value.\n\n**Course:** W4 pp. 12\u201320.\n\n## 3. Policy-weighted value and stochastic branches\n\n**Decode first:** s is the current state; \u03c0 is the policy; $V^\\pi$ means value under that policy; Q is the value of a particular first action. **Method:** multiply each possible value by its probability, then add. In the second part, add the immediate reward separately and discount only the continuation. **Check:** the policy average must lie between its smallest and largest Q-values.\n\nAt $s$, the policy chooses left with probability 0.25 and right with probability 0.75. Their action values are 4 and 8. What is $V^\\pi(s)$? Separately, an action yields reward 2 and then reaches states of value 10 or 0 with equal probability; $\\gamma=0.9$. What is its Q-value?\n\n> [!question]- Show answer\n> $V^\\pi(s)=0.25(4)+0.75(8)=7$. The uniform average 6 is wrong because the policy is not uniform.\n>\n> $Q(s,a)=2+0.9[0.5(10)+0.5(0)]=6.5$.\n>\n> For an optimal choice between action values 4 and 8, choose 8. Random transition outcomes still require expectation; the agent cannot select whichever random successor it prefers.\n\n**Course:** W5 pp. 7\u201319.\n\n## 4. TD versus Monte Carlo\n\n**Decode first:** $s'$ is the next state, not a derivative; V is the current value estimate; $\\alpha$ is update size; $\\gamma$ discounts the continuation. **Method:** write target \u2192 subtract old value \u2192 multiply error by learning rate \u2192 add correction to old value. For MC, replace the TD target with the complete observed return. **Check:** with learning rate 0.1, move only 10% of the distance to the target.\n\nCurrent $V(s)=4$, $V(s')=6$, next reward 2, $\\gamma=0.9$, $\\alpha=0.1$. Calculate the TD target, error and update. If the complete observed return is 10, calculate the MC update instead.\n\n> [!question]- Show answer\n> TD target $=2+0.9(6)=7.4$. TD error $=7.4-4=3.4$. New value $=4+0.1(3.4)=4.34$.\n>\n> MC target $=10$. New value $=4+0.1(10-4)=4.6$.\n>\n> MC waits for the return; TD uses the current successor estimate. Neither target changes the definition of true value.\n\n**Course:** W7 pp. 9\u201316.\n\n## 5. SARSA versus Q-learning\n\n**Decode first:** $Q(s,a)$ is the stored estimate being corrected; the next-state values are possible continuations. **Method:** SARSA uses the value of the actual next action; Q-learning takes the maximum. Then apply the same target-minus-old update to both. **Check:** if the selected action were already greedy, these one-step targets would agree.\n\n$Q(s,a)=5$, reward 2, $\\gamma=0.9$, $\\alpha=0.1$. At the next state, action values are 8, 11 and 5. The next action actually selected has value 5. Calculate both updates.\n\n> [!question]- Show answer\n> SARSA target $=2+0.9(5)=6.5$; updated Q $=5+0.1(6.5-5)=5.15$.\n>\n> Q-learning target $=2+0.9(11)=11.9$; updated Q $=5+0.1(11.9-5)=5.69$.\n>\n> At a terminal successor both targets become 2, giving $Q_{new}=4.7$. Do not maximise over actions that cannot occur after termination.\n\n**Course:** W7 pp. 31\u201343. The successor values echo the lecture mini-example; other numbers are chosen for practice.\n\n## 6. First-visit and every-visit MC\n\n**Decode first:** A and B label states, and the arrows show their visit order. $\\gamma=1$ means no discounting. **Method:** compute a return for each occurrence of A, then decide which occurrences the method keeps. **Check:** the second A cannot receive credit for rewards that arrived before it.\n\nTrajectory: $A\\to B\\to A\\to\\mathrm{terminal}$, rewards 1, 2, 3, $\\gamma=1$. What returns are recorded for A?\n\n> [!question]- Show answer\n> First A return is 6; second A return is 3. First-visit MC records only 6 for A this episode. Every-visit records 6 and 3, averaging to 4.5 if these are the only samples collected.\n\n**Course:** W6 pp. 16\u201323.\n\n## 7. Advantage and REINFORCE sign\n\n**Decode first:** Q is the action value, V the state\u2019s policy average, and A(s,a) the advantage. $G_t$ is sampled return and b is the scalar baseline. **Method:** subtract V from Q for the first part; divide G by 100 before subtracting b for the second. **Check:** compare values in the same units; do not subtract a scaled baseline from an unscaled return.\n\n$Q(s,a)=7$, $V(s)=5$. Find advantage. For the MEDS scaled-return update, suppose $G_t=50$ and baseline $b=0.7$. Does this action get a positive or negative policy-gradient weight, ignoring entropy?\n\n> [!question]- Show answer\n> $A(s,a)=7-5=2$. For the second question, $G_t/100-b=0.5-0.7=-0.2$: a negative weight because the return is below baseline, even though raw return is positive.\n>\n> This is the direction contributed by that sample; shared network parameters and other samples can affect the final probabilities.\n\n**Course:** W10 pp. 22\u201335; textbook \u00a713.4. Scaling: project P3/P5.\n\n## 8. PPO clipping: positive and negative advantages\n\n**Decode first:** $\\rho$ is new probability divided by old probability; $\\epsilon$ is the clipping margin; $\\hat A$ denotes the estimated advantage; min chooses the smaller numerical term. **Method:** compute the raw ratio, clip a second copy, multiply both by the advantage, then take the minimum. **Check:** with negative advantage, more negative is smaller; do not choose by absolute magnitude.\n\nOld action probability 0.2, new probability 0.3, $\\epsilon=0.2$. Compute the ratio and objective term for advantage +2, then for \u22122. Finally use ratio 0.5 and advantage \u22122.\n\n> [!question]- Show answer\n> Ratio $\\rho=0.3/0.2=1.5$; clipped ratio 1.2.\n>\n> For +2: $\\min(1.5\\times2,1.2\\times2)=\\min(3,2.4)=2.4$.\n>\n> For \u22122: $\\min(-3,-2.4)=-3$. Increasing the probability of a bad action is still penalised; taking the minimum is essential.\n>\n> For $\\rho=0.5$, advantage \u22122: clipped ratio 0.8; $\\min(-1,-1.6)=-1.6$. The objective removes the extra incentive for reducing that probability further.\n>\n> These are objective values being maximised, not probabilities or rewards. Clipping does not mean the actual ratio always stays inside the interval.\n\n**Course:** W10 pp. 46\u201351.\n\n## 9. Two-step critic target\n\n**Decode first:** $\\hat G^{(2)}$ is a two-step return estimate; the hat means estimated and the parenthesised 2 is the number of observed rewards. V is the critic\u2019s prediction after those steps. **Method:** first reward + once-discounted second reward + twice-discounted continuation. **Check:** remove the continuation when the second transition truly terminates the task.\n\nRewards 1 and 2, successor critic value 4, $\\gamma=0.9$. Find the two-step target. What if the second transition terminates the episode?\n\n> [!question]- Show answer\n> $\\hat G^{(2)}=1+0.9(2)+0.9^2(4)=6.04$. If truly terminal after the second transition, the target is $1+0.9(2)=2.8$.\n\n**Course:** textbook \u00a77.1; W10 p. 33 for the one-step terminal convention.\n\n## 10. Interpret a results chart\n\n**Decode first:** a mean reward averages reward totals; a delivery count measures task progress. **Method:** separate the metric being optimised from the outcome you want to claim, then compare experimental conditions. **Check:** a prettier reward number cannot establish a delivery improvement when deliveries remain zero.\n\nPolicy X has mean reward \u221225 and zero deliveries. Policy Y has mean reward \u221237 and zero deliveries. Can you claim improved delivery performance? If one evaluation enables traffic and another disables it, can you rank their algorithms?\n\n> [!question]- Show answer\n> No to both. Reward differs, but the primary delivery outcome did not improve. Different traffic conditions confound an algorithm comparison. State the observed outcomes and conditions, then propose a matched evaluation.\n\n**Project:** P4, P6\u2013P8; this is interpretation practice, not an additional experiment.\n\n---\n\n[[04_Project_Defence|Previous]] \u00b7 [[OralDefence/README|Study map]] \u00b7 [[06_Mock_Defence|Next]]\n"}, {"title": "Mock oral defence", "file": "06_Mock_Defence.md", "text": "\n# Mock oral defence\n\n> [!abstract] Study focus\n> Practise a short answer, an equation or example, and a connection to MEDS. These prompts are practice, not an official rubric.\n\n[[OralDefence/README|Study guide]] \u00b7 [[04_Project_Defence|Project evidence]]\n\nUse these as rehearsal questions, not a script to memorise. For each answer: define the concept, give one equation or example, then connect it to MEDS. Aim for 20\u201340 seconds before inviting a follow-up.\n\n## Round 1: foundations\n\n| Question | Minimum good answer | Likely follow-up |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| What is model-free RL? | Learns from experience without a dynamics model used for planning | Why can MEDS still have a simulator? |\n| What is an MDP? | States, actions, transitions, rewards, discount; Markov property | Is your observation sufficient? |\n| What is $G_t$? | Discounted future rewards after action time t | Compute it for three rewards |\n| Difference between $V$ and $Q$? | Value of a state versus a specified first action | How does $\\pi$ connect them? |\n| What is a policy? | A rule/distribution for action selection | Sampled versus greedy evaluation? |\n| Why discount rewards? | Controls how future rewards contribute | What happens at zero or one? |\n| Reward versus return? | Immediate signal versus trajectory aggregate | Why can reward rise without delivery? |\n| Prediction versus control? | Evaluate a fixed policy versus improve behaviour | Policy iteration versus value iteration? |\n\nAnswers and course references: [[01_Foundations|Foundations]], [[02_Learning_Methods|Learning methods]].\n\n## Round 2: algorithms\n\n| Question | Minimum good answer | Likely follow-up |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| What is bootstrapping? | Using an estimated successor value in a target | Does REINFORCE bootstrap? |\n| MC versus TD? | Complete return versus reward plus estimated continuation | Explain bias and variance |\n| How does Q-learning change the return target? | Uses reward plus maximum next Q | Does the definition of return change? |\n| SARSA versus Q-learning? | Chosen next action versus greedy next-action target | Why does exploration matter near a cliff? |\n| What is REINFORCE? | On-policy MC policy gradient | Trace one batch through your code |\n| Why subtract a baseline? | Reduce variance without changing expected score contribution when action-independent | Is your baseline a critic? |\n| What is actor\u2013critic? | Actor policy plus value estimator guiding updates | What is the TD error? |\n| What is advantage? | Q minus policy-weighted state value | Can a negative Q have positive advantage? |\n| What is PPO? | Policy optimisation with clipped probability-ratio surrogate | Work through negative advantage |\n| Why use an MLP here? | Input is a compact numerical vector | What would justify a CNN? |\n| Why entropy regularisation? | Encourages a broader action distribution | Does that guarantee useful exploration? |\n| Why replay and target networks in DQN? | Reduce correlation/reuse data; stabilise target | Why not blindly replay old data in REINFORCE? |\n\nAnswers and course references: [[02_Learning_Methods|Learning methods]], [[03_Policy_Gradients|Policy gradients]].\n\n## Round 3: defend your project\n\n1. **Describe one successful episode.** Include all three clinic\u2013hospital deliveries, return to start and Stop. Pickup alone is insufficient.\n2. **What does the policy actually observe?** Describe the five observation groups; say that rendering is for visualisation.\n3. **Why might the observation be incomplete?** Mention internal visited-cell history and traffic details; separate the concern from measured causal evidence.\n4. **What were your exact REINFORCE settings?** Name the saved run, discount, learning rate, batch, baseline and evaluation conditions.\n5. **How did you evaluate?** Fixed checkpoint, no learning, sampled and greedy modes, 20 episodes each, stated seeds and traffic setting.\n6. **What improved?** Be precise: greedy reward and invalid-action count differed; final pickups/deliveries/full success remained zero. Earlier tuning pickup findings are a separate study.\n7. **Why not declare PPO best?** No-traffic versus traffic conditions and incomplete A2C provenance prevent a controlled ranking.\n8. **What evidence supports failure?** The final evaluation JSON records zero task outcomes and traffic-collision endings in every episode.\n9. **Why are training pickups different from evaluation pickups?** Different policies and exploration contexts; final evaluation measures the selected frozen checkpoint.\n10. **What would you test next?** One explicit hypothesis, one controlled change, matched budgets, seeds and primary task metrics. Label it proposed.\n11. **How do you know your implementation is correct?** Explain return/loss checks and artifact inspection, then acknowledge that code checks do not prove task performance.\n12. **Which part was yours?** Describe actual work and explain it through concrete functions. Do not claim ownership of another group member\u2019s code.\n\nEvidence-backed answers: [[04_Project_Defence|Project defence]].\n\n## Two timed rehearsals\n\n**Rehearsal A, about ten minutes:** give the 45-second project explanation; answer model-free/MDP/V-versus-Q; work calculations 1 and 5; compare REINFORCE to actor\u2013critic; explain the final results and one limitation.\n\n**Rehearsal B, about ten minutes:** point to the PPO equation and name every symbol; work calculation 8; trace observation \u2192 logits \u2192 action \u2192 return \u2192 loss \u2192 update; challenge the Markov assumption; defend the algorithm-comparison caveat.\n\nHave a teammate interrupt with \u201cwhy?\u201d or \u201cshow me in the code.\u201d Score each answer yourself: **0** incorrect/absent; **1** definition only; **2** correct explanation plus equation/example; **3** also connected to MEDS and its evidence. This is a study aid, not the course marking scheme.\n\n## If you get stuck\n\n\u201cI know the target is immediate reward plus a discounted continuation estimate. Let me identify whether this is the chosen next action or the maximum next Q.\u201d\n\n\u201cI can establish the measured outcome, but I cannot establish that causal explanation from this experiment.\u201d\n\n\u201cI would check the saved configuration and checkpoint rather than infer the setting from the current notebook defaults.\u201d\n\nThese are useful only when followed by real reasoning or evidence. Practise until you can explain the concepts without these prompts.\n\n---\n\n[[05_Worked_Calculations|Previous]] \u00b7 [[OralDefence/README|Study map]] \u00b7 [[07_Sources_and_Presentation_Checklist|Next]]\n"}, {"title": "Source map and presentation checklist", "file": "07_Sources_and_Presentation_Checklist.md", "text": "\n# Source map and presentation checklist\n\n> [!abstract] Study focus\n> Use W references for course theory and P references for project evidence. PDF page positions may differ from printed slide numbers.\n\n[[OralDefence/README|Study guide]]\n\n## How to use the references\n\nW1\u2013W11 below refer only to files inside the requested `ReinforcementL/Materials/` directory. Page references in the notes are **one-based PDF page positions**, not the printed footer numbers (these differ in some lectures, particularly Week 7). Course references establish theory; P1\u2013P8 establish project claims. Worked examples are original exercises derived from those equations.\n\nNo outside web sources or pre-existing notes outside `Materials/` were used as course-theory sources. DeepSeek Flash assisted bounded reading; the key formulas, project code and final configuration/evaluation claims were checked against local sources. PPO pp. 46\u201347 were inspected as images because text extraction omitted their equations.\n\n## Course PDFs\n\n| ID | Local source | Relevant subjects |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| W1 | [[ReinforcementL/Materials/43008-Reinforcement-Learning---Spring-2026-2026-Nov-04_03-12-51-434/viewer/files/Modules/Week1/Lecture/Week1-Lecture-New-2026.pdf\\|Week1-Lecture-New-2026]] | RL framework, rewards, exploration |\n| W2 | [[ReinforcementL/Materials/43008-Reinforcement-Learning---Spring-2026-2026-Nov-04_03-12-51-434/viewer/files/Modules/Week2/Lecture/Week2-Lecture-2024.pdf\\|Week2-Lecture-2024]] | State/observation, POMDPs, bandits |\n| W3 | [[ReinforcementL/Materials/43008-Reinforcement-Learning---Spring-2026-2026-Nov-04_03-12-51-434/viewer/files/Modules/Week3/Lecture/Week3-Lecture-2026.pdf\\|Week3-Lecture-2026]] | MDPs, returns, values |\n| W4 | [[ReinforcementL/Materials/43008-Reinforcement-Learning---Spring-2026-2026-Nov-04_03-12-51-434/viewer/files/Modules/Week4/Lecture/Week4-Lecture-2026.pdf\\|Week4-Lecture-2026]] | Bellman equations and policy iteration |\n| W5 | [[ReinforcementL/Materials/43008-Reinforcement-Learning---Spring-2026-2026-Nov-04_03-12-51-434/viewer/files/Modules/Week5/Lecture/Week5-Lecture-2026.pdf\\|Week5-Lecture-2026]] | Bellman relationships and value iteration |\n| W6 | [[ReinforcementL/Materials/43008-Reinforcement-Learning---Spring-2026-2026-Nov-04_03-12-51-434/viewer/files/Modules/Week6/Lecture/Week6-Lecture-2026.pdf\\|Week6-Lecture-2026]] | Monte Carlo, on/off-policy learning |\n| W7 | [[ReinforcementL/Materials/43008-Reinforcement-Learning---Spring-2026-2026-Nov-04_03-12-51-434/viewer/files/Modules/Week7/Lecture/Week7-Lecture-2026-v2.pdf\\|Week7-Lecture-2026-v2]] | TD, SARSA and Q-learning |\n| W8 | [[ReinforcementL/Materials/43008-Reinforcement-Learning---Spring-2026-2026-Nov-04_03-12-51-434/viewer/files/Modules/Week8/Lecture/Week8-Lecture-2025.pdf\\|Week8-Lecture-2025]] | Function approximation |\n| W9 | [[ReinforcementL/Materials/43008-Reinforcement-Learning---Spring-2026-2026-Nov-04_03-12-51-434/viewer/files/Modules/Week9/Lecture/Week9-Lecture-2023.pdf\\|Week9-Lecture-2023]] | DQN, replay and target networks |\n| W10 | [[ReinforcementL/Materials/43008-Reinforcement-Learning---Spring-2026-2026-Nov-04_03-12-51-434/viewer/files/Modules/Week10/Lecture/Week10-Lecture-2024.pdf\\|Week10-Lecture-2024]] | REINFORCE, actor\u2013critic, advantage and PPO |\n| W11 | [[ReinforcementL/Materials/43008-Reinforcement-Learning---Spring-2026-2026-Nov-04_03-12-51-434/viewer/files/Modules/Week11/Lecture/Week11-Lecture-2025.pdf\\|Week11-Lecture-2025]] | Model-based learning and planning |\n\n**Textbook:** [[ReinforcementL/Materials/RLbook2020.pdf|Sutton and Barto, local RLbook2020.pdf]] \u2014 \u00a7\u00a73.3\u20133.5 (returns/values), 5.1 (MC), 6.1\u20136.5 (TD/control), 7.1 (n-step targets), 13.1\u201313.5 (policy gradients, baseline and actor\u2013critic). Use section numbers because PDF and printed pages differ.\n\n**Implementation reading in the course collection:**\n\n- [[ReinforcementL/Materials/43008-Reinforcement-Learning---Spring-2026-2026-Nov-04_03-12-51-434/viewer/files/Modules/Week10/Lab/2025/43008-Week10-Part-A-ActorCritic-BiPedalWalker-Solution.ipynb|43008-Week10-Part-A-ActorCritic-BiPedalWalker-Solution.ipynb]]\n- [[ReinforcementL/Materials/43008-Reinforcement-Learning---Spring-2026-2026-Nov-04_03-12-51-434/viewer/files/Modules/Week10/Lab/2025/43008-Week10-Part-B-PPO-BiPedalWalker-Solution.ipynb|43008-Week10-Part-B-PPO-BiPedalWalker-Solution.ipynb]]\n- [[ReinforcementL/Materials/43008-Reinforcement-Learning---Spring-2026-2026-Nov-04_03-12-51-434/viewer/files/Modules/Week10/Lab/2025/43008-Week10-Part-C-PPO-Atari-CustomCNN-Solution-Colab.ipynb|43008-Week10-Part-C-PPO-Atari-CustomCNN-Solution-Colab.ipynb]]\n\nThe Week 10 labs connect the lecture concepts to Stable-Baselines3 actor\u2013critic/PPO implementations. They are supplementary reading, not evidence that MEDS used identical defaults. Weeks 11\u201312 topics such as RLHF and hierarchical RL are lower priority for this presentation; study them further if required by an official rubric.\n\n## Project evidence\n\n- **P1:** [[OralDefence/Project Grup 11 -JustInTime.pdf|Presentation]]\n- **P2:** [[final_report/1.4_Dataset_and_Environment|Environment description]]\n- **P3:** [[final_report/3.1.4_REINFORCE_Settings|REINFORCE settings]]\n- **P4:** [[final_report/3.3.3_REINFORCE_Results|REINFORCE results and tuning comparisons]]\n- **P5:** [[final_report/reinforce_evidence/sources/MEDS_REINFORCE_v1_2_Tuning.ipynb|Archived REINFORCE implementation]]\n- **P6:** [[final_report/reinforce_evidence/sources/20261026T025626_518818Z_combined_seed42/results/evaluation_004000054_final.json|Final evaluation JSON]]\n- **P7:** [[Assignment-3-PartE-SuthiwatPraesi-24770176.pdf|Submitted Part E report]]\n- **P8:** [[OralDefence/plot_results.py|Presentation graph source]]\n\n- [[final_report/reinforce_evidence/sources/20261026T025626_518818Z_combined_seed42/config.json|Saved REINFORCE configuration]]\n- [[final_report/2.4.3_REINFORCE|Implemented loss explanation]]\n- [[final_report/reinforce_evidence/sources/New_testing_MEDS_Environment_1410.ipynb|Archived supplied environment]]\n- [[OralDefence/example|Anecdotal oral-defence example]]\n\n## Presentation alignment and unresolved items\n\nThis checklist refers to the supplied 12-page presentation PDF. It records preparation needs; the PDF itself was not edited.\n\n| PDF page | Current content | Preparation needed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Title | Give a one-sentence definition of MEDS |\n| 2\u20133 | Motivation and business problem | Separate real-world motivation from demonstrated simulation outcomes; substantiate contextual examples separately if retained |\n| 4 | Observations and actions | Explain the 17 components and eight actions; correct clinic C index to 2 and clarify sentinel 3; clarify completion flags |\n| 5 | Rewards | Check each reward branch against the environment version; avoid double-counting delivery rewards or claiming a component is the full step reward |\n| 6 | GUI and interaction loop | Explain reset/step/render; distinguish observation from display |\n| 7 | A2C and settings | Explain actor, critic, advantage and rollout length; verify settings against the saved A2C run |\n| 8 | PPO placeholder | Add ratio/clipped objective, actual settings and a cautious result statement |\n| 9 | REINFORCE placeholder | Add complete-return update, scalar EMA baseline, selected settings and final task outcomes |\n| 10 | Demo labels | Identify each demo\u2019s checkpoint/settings; distinguish an illustration from evaluation evidence |\n| 11 | Generic chart and placeholder results text | Replace placeholders with sourced task outcomes; the existing results graph is available, but retain its unequal-condition warning |\n| 12 | Placeholder conclusion | State partial progress, no demonstrated full-task solution, comparison limits and one proposed next experiment |\n\n### Claims to avoid\n\n- \u201cThe best reward means the best delivery policy.\u201d Check task outcomes.\n- \u201cPPO beat REINFORCE.\u201d Evaluation conditions differ.\n- \u201cA2C\u2019s mean pickup count is a percentage.\u201d The quantity and denominator are not established for that conversion.\n- \u201cThe 4M run confirms the 40M tuning improvement.\u201d Different budgets and experiments.\n- \u201cA scalar baseline is our trained critic.\u201d The selected baseline is an EMA scalar.\n- \u201cPPO clips every actual probability ratio.\u201d It clips a term in the objective.\n- \u201cThe observation is Markov because it has 17 features.\u201d Sufficiency depends on information, not dimension.\n- \u201cA negative entropy loss proves no exploration.\u201d Action entropy alone does not establish state coverage.\n- \u201cLoss magnitudes prove the policy understands or does not understand the task.\u201d Interpret behaviour and evaluation outcomes directly.\n\n### Remaining evidence gaps\n\nA2C\u2019s evaluation denominator and final checkpoint identity need confirmation for a controlled comparison. PPO results here are report-backed rather than independently recomputed. The reported REINFORCE seeds were reused for selection and do not constitute held-out testing; fixed resets do not vary layouts. No new evaluation was performed while building these notes.\n\n---\n\n[[06_Mock_Defence|Previous]] \u00b7 [[OralDefence/README|Study map]] \u00b7 [[OralDefence/README|Next]]\n"}, {"title": "REINFORCE explained: intuition, equations and MEDS code", "file": "08_REINFORCE_Explained.md", "text": "\n# REINFORCE explained: intuition, equations and MEDS code\n\n[[OralDefence/README|Study map]] \u00b7 [[03_Policy_Gradients|Compare policy-gradient methods]] \u00b7 [[07_Sources_and_Presentation_Checklist|Sources]]\n\n> [!abstract] The idea in one sentence\n> Try actions, observe what happens afterwards, and adjust the policy so actions associated with better-than-reference outcomes become more likely.\n\nRead sections 1\u20135 first. Then connect the baseline, entropy and loss to the code in sections 6\u201311. Every displayed equation below has a symbol explanation. Numerical examples are teaching examples, not measured MEDS results.\n\n## 1. What does REINFORCE learn?\n\nIt learns a **policy**: a rule that assigns probabilities to actions. It does not need a correct-action label for each situation or a model of every possible next state.\n\nFor MEDS, the policy receives 17 numbers describing the vehicle and task. Its network has two hidden layers of 128 units and produces eight action scores. These scores are called **logits**. A categorical distribution turns them into probabilities and samples an action.\n\n$$p_t=\\pi_\\theta(a_t\\mid o_t).$$\n\n| Symbol | Meaning in plain language |\n| --- | --- |\n| $t$ | Current step within the episode; counting starts at zero |\n| $o_t$ | Observation available to the agent at this step |\n| $a_t$ | Action selected at this step |\n| $\\pi$ | The policy: the action-selection rule |\n| $\\theta$ | All adjustable weights and biases in the policy network |\n| Subscript $\\theta$ | Reminds us that changing the weights changes the policy |\n| $\\mid$ | Read as \u201cgiven\u201d; it is not division |\n| $p_t$ | Probability the policy assigns to this selected action |\n\n**Read aloud:** \u201cThe probability of choosing this action, given this observation, under our current network.\u201d\n\nAt a clinic, the policy might assign Pickup a probability of 0.2. Sampling means Pickup is possible but not guaranteed. Greedy selection instead chooses whichever action has the largest probability.\n\n> [!note] State versus observation\n> Course equations usually use $s_t$ or $S_t$ for state. This note uses $o_t$ when describing the actual MEDS input. The supplied observation is not proven to contain the full Markov state. This notation keeps that limitation visible.\n\nThe code also divides each observation component by its configured maximum category index, with a minimum divisor of one. For example, a coordinate ranging from 0 to 14 is divided by 14. That input scaling is separate from dividing returns by 100.\n\n**Sources:** W10 pp. 5\u201312; textbook \u00a713.1; archived notebook `ReinforcePolicy` (P5).\n\n## 2. Reward is immediate; return looks ahead\n\nA reward says what happened after one action. A return combines the rewards that followed an action throughout the rest of the episode.\n\n$$G_t=\\sum_{k=t}^{T-1}\\gamma^{k-t}r_k.$$\n\n| Symbol | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| $G_t$ | Reward-to-go: the future score assigned to the action at step $t$ |\n| $\\sum$ | \u201cAdd all the following terms\u201d |\n| $k$ | A counting variable that visits the current and later steps |\n| $k=t$ | Start with the reward immediately after the current action |\n| $T$ | Number of actions in the episode |\n| $T-1$ | Index of the last action, because indexing starts at zero |\n| $r_k$ | Reward returned after action $a_k$; the notebook stores this as `rewards[k]` |\n| $\\gamma$ | Discount factor; MEDS uses 0.99 |\n| $\\gamma^{k-t}$ | How much to reduce the weight of a reward that arrives $k-t$ steps later |\n\n**Read aloud:** \u201cAdd the rewards from now to the end, giving later rewards less weight.\u201d\n\nThe course writes the reward after action $A_t$ as $R_{t+1}$. Here it is $r_t$. These are two indexing conventions for the same feedback, not different algorithms.\n\n### Small numerical example\n\nImagine three rewards: 2, \u22121, 5. Use discount 0.9 for easy arithmetic.\n\n| Action time | Rewards still ahead | Return |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 2 | 5 | 5 |\n| 1 | \u22121, then 5 | \u22121 + 0.9 \u00d7 5 = 3.5 |\n| 0 | 2, then \u22121, then 5 | 2 + 0.9 \u00d7 (\u22121) + 0.81 \u00d7 5 = 5.15 |\n\nThe last reward influences earlier actions. This is how a later delivery reward can reinforce earlier navigation and pickup actions.\n\n**Why exclude rewards before the action?** An action cannot cause rewards already received. Reward-to-go removes this irrelevant past-reward noise from its learning signal.\n\n### How the code calculates it\n\n$$G_t=r_t+\\gamma G_{t+1},\\qquad G_T=0.$$\n\n- $G_{t+1}$: return already calculated for the next step.\n- $r_t$: current reward.\n- $\\gamma$: discount applied once to the remaining future.\n- $G_T=0$: there are no observed rewards after the episode boundary.\n\nStart at the end and move backwards. `reward_to_go` implements exactly this recurrence. In the reported implementation, even a step-limit truncation uses zero continuation rather than a value bootstrap. That describes its finite recorded return; it does not imply that an artificial time limit is always equivalent to a true terminal state.\n\n**Sources:** W3 pp. 17\u201321; W10 pp. 22\u201324; textbook \u00a713.3; P5 `reward_to_go`.\n\n## 3. What is the policy trying to maximise?\n\n$$J(\\theta)=\\mathbb E_{\\tau\\sim\\pi_\\theta}[G_0].$$\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| $J$ | The objective: expected score at the start of an episode |\n| $\\theta$ | The network parameters we can change |\n| $\\mathbb E$ | Expected value: an average over possible episodes, not just one lucky episode |\n| $\\tau$ | One trajectory: the sequence of observations, actions and rewards |\n| $\\sim$ | \u201cSampled according to\u201d |\n| $\\pi_\\theta$ | The policy used to generate the actions |\n| $G_0$ | Discounted return beginning at step zero |\n\n**Read aloud:** \u201cFind network settings that produce a higher average discounted episode return.\u201d\n\nThis is the basic task-return objective. Our implementation adds entropy regularisation to encourage action diversity, so its optimisation loss contains an additional term.\n\n> [!warning] Expected reward is not the same as medical-delivery success\n> If shaping rewards can be obtained without delivery, a better return need not mean the task was completed. That is why the MEDS report also measures pickups, deliveries and full success.\n\n**Sources:** W10 pp. 13\u201316; textbook \u00a7\u00a713.1\u201313.3; P4 for the project distinction.\n\n## 4. Why use a log probability and a gradient?\n\nThe policy must discover which weights to change. A **gradient** is a vector of sensitivities: one component for each adjustable parameter.\n\n$$g_t=\\nabla_\\theta\\log\\pi_\\theta(a_t\\mid o_t).$$\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| $g_t$ | A vector pointing toward increasing the selected action\u2019s log probability locally |\n| $\\nabla_\\theta$ | Differentiate with respect to every network weight and bias |\n| $\\log$ | Natural logarithm; PyTorch uses this convention |\n| $\\pi_\\theta(a_t\\mid o_t)$ | Probability of the action that was actually sampled |\n\nThe gradient is **not** the return, probability, or loss. It tells us how to change parameters; the return-based weight tells us whether and how strongly to follow that direction.\n\n### The useful calculus identity\n\n$$\\nabla_\\theta\\log p_\\theta=\\frac{\\nabla_\\theta p_\\theta}{p_\\theta}.$$\n\nHere $p_\\theta$ is a positive action probability, $\\nabla_\\theta p_\\theta$ is its sensitivity to the weights, and dividing by $p_\\theta$ gives the log-probability gradient. This is the score-function identity from the course. It allows a sampled action to contribute a policy-gradient estimate.\n\nWe do **not** need to differentiate through the simulator\u2019s movement, collisions or reward code. We differentiate the policy\u2019s log probability and use the observed return as feedback.\n\n### Why a negative log probability is normal\n\nThe natural log of 0.2 is about \u22121.609; the log of 0.3 is about \u22121.204. Raising probability raises log probability even though both numbers are negative. A negative log probability is not evidence that the action is bad.\n\n**Sources:** W10 pp. 17\u201323; textbook \u00a7\u00a713.2\u201313.3. Numerical log values are illustrative.\n\n## 5. The basic REINFORCE update, piece by piece\n\n$$\\theta_{\\mathrm{new}}=\\theta_{\\mathrm{old}}+\n\\alpha\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\\gamma^tG_tg_t.$$\n\n| Piece | Role |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\theta_{\\mathrm{old}}$, $\\theta_{\\mathrm{new}}$ | Network parameters before and after the illustrative update |\n| $+$ | Gradient ascent: move toward a larger objective |\n| $\\alpha$ | Learning rate; controls the update size |\n| $\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}$ | Collect contributions from every action in the episode |\n| $\\gamma^t$ | Weight the step relative to the start of the episode |\n| $G_t$ | Reward-to-go for that action |\n| $g_t$ | Log-probability gradient defined in section 4 |\n\n**Read aloud:** \u201cFor each action, take the direction that increases its probability, weight that direction by its future return and discount, then combine the contributions.\u201d\n\nThis is an illustrative accumulated gradient-ascent update. Our code batches episodes and uses Adam, so the exact parameter change is not simply learning rate multiplied by this raw vector.\n\n### Why is discount used twice?\n\nInside $G_t$, discount compares future rewards with the time of the action. The outer factor compares the action\u2019s time with the start of the episode.\n\n$$\\gamma^t\\gamma^{k-t}=\\gamma^k.$$\n\nHere $t$ is the action time and $k$ is the reward time. The exponents add, so the reward receives its intended start-relative discount. This is not two unrelated discount parameters. W10 p. 24 includes the outer factor; some overview formulas abbreviate it.\n\n### A tiny gradient example\n\nSuppose there is one adjustable parameter, the log-probability gradient is 0.4, the return is 5, the learning rate is 0.01, and this is step zero. The contribution is 0.01 \u00d7 1 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 0.4 = +0.02. A return of \u22125 would reverse that contribution.\n\nReal networks have many weights and combine many samples. A positive contribution for one action does not guarantee its final probability rises after every combined update.\n\n**Sources:** W10 pp. 22\u201324; textbook \u00a713.3. The numerical example uses simple gradient ascent, not an exact Adam calculation.\n\n## 6. Baseline: ask \u201cbetter than what?\u201d\n\nA baseline provides a comparison level. The MEDS code first scales the return, then subtracts a scalar baseline:\n\n$$w_t=G_t/c-b.$$\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| $w_t$ | Weight assigned to the sampled action\u2019s log-probability gradient |\n| $G_t$ | Original discounted return |\n| $c$ | Positive return divisor; 100 in the reported run |\n| $b$ | Scalar comparison level, in the same scaled units |\n\nI call this $w_t$ a **return weight**, not the exact state-dependent advantage. The scalar baseline does not generally equal the value of this particular observation.\n\n| Return | Divisor | Baseline | Weight | Interpretation |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| 50 | 100 | 0.3 | +0.2 | Better than the reference |\n| 50 | 100 | 0.7 | \u22120.2 | Worse than the reference |\n| \u221220 | 100 | \u22120.5 | +0.3 | Negative outcome, but better than the reference |\n\n**Why can this help?** Episode outcomes vary because actions are sampled and trajectories differ. Comparing against a sensible reference can reduce variation in the update signal. This is variance reduction, not removal of every source of randomness.\n\n### Why an action-independent baseline does not change the expected policy gradient\n\nFor a fixed observation:\n\n$$\\sum_a\\pi_\\theta(a\\mid o)\\,b\\,\\nabla_\\theta\\log\\pi_\\theta(a\\mid o)\n=b\\,\\nabla_\\theta\\sum_a\\pi_\\theta(a\\mid o)\n=b\\,\\nabla_\\theta1=0.$$\n\n- $\\sum_a$: consider every possible action.\n- $\\pi_\\theta(a\\mid o)$: weight each action by how likely it is.\n- $b$: the same baseline for all those actions, treated as fixed during this derivative.\n- $\\nabla_\\theta$: the sensitivity to network parameters.\n- $1$: all action probabilities sum to one.\n- $0$: the derivative of that constant total is zero.\n\n**Plain meaning:** the baseline\u2019s extra contribution cancels on average across sampled actions. It changes the variability of estimates without adding an expected score contribution. This does not mean it leaves each individual update unchanged, or that every possible baseline reduces variance.\n\n### Our moving-average baseline\n\n$$b_{\\mathrm{new}}=d\\,b_{\\mathrm{old}}+(1-d)\\bar u.$$\n\n| Symbol | Role in this run |\n| --- | --- |\n| $b_{\\mathrm{old}}$ | Baseline used for the current policy update |\n| $b_{\\mathrm{new}}$ | Baseline saved for the next update |\n| $d$ | Decay, set to 0.9: retain 90% of the earlier baseline |\n| $1-d$ | Weight on the new batch information, here 0.1 |\n| $\\bar u$ | Mean of all scaled reward-to-go values from all timesteps in the batch |\n\nIf the earlier baseline is 0.3 and the batch mean is 0.5, the new baseline is 0.9 \u00d7 0.3 + 0.1 \u00d7 0.5 = 0.32. It starts at zero and is updated **after** the policy update.\n\nNote the averaging distinction: the baseline mean pools timestep returns; the policy loss averages episode sums. Neither is simply the average of episode-total rewards.\n\n> [!important] Our baseline is not an actor\u2013critic value network\n> It is one moving-average number. It does not predict a different value for each observation, and the return target does not bootstrap. The archived notebook supports an optional learned baseline, but the reported run selects `ema`.\n\n**Sources:** textbook \u00a713.4; P3/P5, `policy_loss_terms`, `baseline_predictions`, `fit_baseline` and `update_baseline`.\n\n## 7. Loss: convert \u201cincrease good choices\u201d into minimisation\n\nFor one sampled action, temporarily leave out entropy:\n\n$$\\ell_t=-\\gamma^t\\log(p_t)w_t.$$\n\n- $\\ell_t$: this step\u2019s contribution to the loss.\n- Leading minus: converts a quantity we want to maximise into a loss to minimise.\n- $\\gamma^t$: start-relative discount.\n- $p_t$: selected-action probability from section 1.\n- $w_t$: scaled return minus baseline from section 6.\n- Multiplication: makes the log-probability learning signal stronger or weaker, or reverses it.\n\nIf $w_t$ is positive, increasing the selected action\u2019s probability lowers this loss. If it is negative, decreasing that probability lowers the loss. Returns and baselines are **detached**: treated as fixed feedback, rather than functions for the optimiser to change to make the loss look smaller.\n\nThe loss is a tool for obtaining a gradient. It is not a calibrated measure of delivery quality, and its absolute value need not improve monotonically as the policy learns.\n\n**Sources:** P5 `policy_loss_terms`; W10 pp. 16\u201324 for ascent and policy gradients.\n\n## 8. Entropy: encourage a spread of action probabilities\n\n$$H_t=-\\sum_{a\\in\\mathcal A}p(a\\mid o_t)\\log p(a\\mid o_t).$$\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| $H_t$ | Entropy of the action distribution at this observation |\n| $\\mathcal A$ | Set of the eight available actions |\n| $a\\in\\mathcal A$ | Consider each action in that set |\n| $p(a\\mid o_t)$ | Probability assigned to that action |\n| $\\log p(a\\mid o_t)$ | Natural log of its probability |\n| Minus and sum | Produce a nonnegative measure of how spread out the distribution is |\n\nA nearly deterministic choice has entropy near zero. A uniform distribution over eight actions has entropy about 2.079, its maximum for these eight choices. The limiting contribution of a zero-probability action is defined as zero.\n\nThe loss subtracts an entropy bonus, so minimising it encourages higher entropy. The coefficient is 0.01 in our run. This competes with the return-weighted policy term; it does not command uniformly random actions forever.\n\n**MEDS limitation:** more diverse actions can include invalid Pickup or Deliver attempts. High action entropy does not guarantee visiting useful states or discovering a successful sequence.\n\n**Source:** entropy use and coefficient are project implementation facts, P3/P5 `train_reinforce`; the formula explains the categorical entropy calculated there. It is an extension to the basic REINFORCE rule discussed in the course.\n\n## 9. The full MEDS loss, now assembled\n\n$$\\mathcal L(\\theta)=-\\frac1B\\sum_{i=1}^{B}\\sum_{t=0}^{T_i-1}\\gamma^t\n\\left[\\log\\pi_\\theta(a_{i,t}\\mid o_{i,t})\n\\left(\\frac{G_{i,t}}c-b\\right)+\\beta H_{i,t}\\right].$$\n\n| Symbol or operation | What to say aloud |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\mathcal L(\\theta)$ | Total batch loss used to update the policy weights |\n| $B$ | Number of complete episodes in this batch, up to eight |\n| $i$ | Episode index within the batch |\n| $T_i$ | Number of actions in episode i |\n| $t$ | Step index within that episode |\n| $\\sum_t$ | Add the step contributions within one episode |\n| $\\sum_i$ and $1/B$ | Add episode losses and average across episodes |\n| $\\gamma^t$ | Discount the whole step contribution, including entropy |\n| $\\pi_\\theta$ | Policy parameterised by the network weights |\n| $a_{i,t}$, $o_{i,t}$ | Sampled action and its observation in episode i, step t |\n| $G_{i,t}$ | Reward-to-go from that action |\n| $c=100$ | Return scaling divisor |\n| $b$ | Earlier scalar moving-average baseline, shared across this batch |\n| $\\beta=0.01$ | Entropy bonus coefficient |\n| $H_{i,t}$ | Entropy across all eight actions at that observation |\n| Leading minus | Turn the maximisation surrogate into a minimisation loss |\n\n**Read aloud:** \u201cFor each step, weight its action log probability by scaled return above baseline, add an entropy bonus, discount it, sum across the episode, and average the episode losses. Negate this because the optimiser minimises.\u201d\n\n### One complete numerical pass through the equation\n\nUse one three-step episode, rewards 2, \u22121, 5 and discount 0.9. Suppose selected-action probabilities are 0.5, 0.25 and 0.5; return divisor is 100; baseline is 0.02.\n\n| Step | Return | Scaled return | Return weight | Log probability | Outer discount | Policy loss contribution |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 0 | 5.15 | 0.0515 | 0.0315 | \u22120.693147 | 1 | 0.021834 |\n| 1 | 3.5 | 0.035 | 0.015 | \u22121.386294 | 0.9 | 0.018715 |\n| 2 | 5 | 0.05 | 0.03 | \u22120.693147 | 0.81 | 0.016843 |\n\nTheir policy-loss sum is approximately **0.057393**. If the full action distributions each have entropy 1.5 and the coefficient is 0.01, the entropy bonus is 0.01 \u00d7 (1 + 0.9 + 0.81) \u00d7 1.5 = **0.04065**. The combined loss is therefore approximately **0.016743**.\n\nEntropy is stipulated separately for this example: you cannot infer the entropy of all eight actions from the selected action\u2019s probability alone. This calculation produces a loss value, not the next network weights; the weights also depend on derivatives and Adam\u2019s stored state.\n\n**Source:** P5 `train_reinforce`, P3 and the implemented-loss explanation linked in the source map.\n\n## 10. Follow one update through the notebook\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[Collect up to eight complete episodes] --> B[Compute reward-to-go backwards]\n    B --> C[Divide returns by 100]\n    C --> D[Subtract the earlier scalar baseline]\n    D --> E[Form discounted log-probability loss and entropy bonus]\n    E --> F[Average episode losses]\n    F --> G[Backpropagate and clip gradient norm]\n    G --> H[Adam updates policy weights]\n    H --> I[Update scalar baseline from this batch]\n    I --> J[Record metrics and collect fresh episodes]\n```\n\n| Function / code operation | Purpose | Oral-defence explanation |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `ReinforcePolicy.forward` | Scale inputs and compute logits | \u201cThe network turns the observation into action scores.\u201d |\n| `collect_episode` | Sample actions until termination/truncation | \u201cWe obtain experience under the current policy.\u201d |\n| `reward_to_go` | Backward discounted accumulation | \u201cEach action receives credit from subsequent rewards.\u201d |\n| `baseline_predictions` | Supply the earlier scalar baseline | \u201cWe compare against a reference from previous batches.\u201d |\n| `policy_loss_terms` | Compute detached return-weighted log-probability terms | \u201cFeedback weights the policy gradient.\u201d |\n| `train_reinforce` | Combine losses, entropy, backpropagation and update | \u201cWe apply one policy update after collecting the batch.\u201d |\n| `clip_grad_norm_` | Limit raw gradient norm to 1.0 | \u201cWe reduce excessively large raw gradients.\u201d |\n| `optimizer.step` | Apply Adam with learning rate 0.001 | \u201cAdam uses gradient history to adjust the weights.\u201d |\n| `fit_baseline` | Update EMA after the policy update | \u201cThe next batch gets an updated reference.\u201d |\n| `evaluate_checkpoint` | Test a saved policy without learning | \u201cWe measure behaviour of a fixed checkpoint.\u201d |\n\nGradient-norm clipping is **not** PPO probability-ratio clipping. With Adam, it also does not directly guarantee a bound on the final parameter-step norm.\n\nThe code recomputes log probabilities for the stored observations/actions before updating. Because the policy has not changed during collection of this batch, those log probabilities refer to the collecting policy. It does not use unrestricted old-trajectory replay.\n\n**Source:** archived notebook P5; settings P3. No code or training was changed to create this note.\n\n## 11. Why learning can be difficult in MEDS\n\n**Delayed credit:** navigation actions may only be useful because a delivery happens much later. The same early action can be followed by very different outcomes, making the sampled return noisy.\n\n**Rare task sequences:** Pickup, correct navigation, Park and Deliver must occur in a useful order. Random action diversity does not ensure this sequence is discovered before collision or timeout.\n\n**Shared scalar baseline:** one number cannot adapt to the different expected returns of every observation. It is simple, but may provide a less informative comparison than a suitable state-dependent baseline.\n\n**Return scaling and entropy:** dividing returns by 100 reduces their magnitude relative to a fixed entropy coefficient. This changes the balance of the two gradient contributions; it is not merely a display conversion.\n\n**Observation limitations:** hidden history and incomplete traffic information can make the same input correspond to different underlying situations.\n\nThese are mechanisms and hypotheses, not isolated causes proven by the run. The measured final outcome remains zero pickups, deliveries and full successes in both 20-episode evaluation modes. See [[04_Project_Defence#What the results actually show|the evidence and limitations]].\n\n**Sources:** W10 p. 28, textbook \u00a7\u00a713.3\u201313.5; P2\u2013P6 for project facts.\n\n## 12. Questions to practise aloud\n\n> [!question]- Why is REINFORCE called Monte Carlo?\n> It learns from sampled complete-episode returns rather than replacing the future with a bootstrapped value estimate. Our recorded step-limit episodes also use only observed rewards.\n\n> [!question]- Does a baseline make it actor\u2013critic?\n> Not by itself. Our baseline is one EMA scalar. Even a learned baseline fitted to complete Monte Carlo returns does not make the policy target bootstrapped. The course distinguishes this from a bootstrapping critic.\n\n> [!question]- Why does the log probability appear?\n> Its derivative tells us how to change policy parameters to change the probability of a sampled action. Weighting that score by return gives the policy-gradient learning signal without differentiating through the environment.\n\n> [!question]- Why can a positive reward lead to a negative update weight?\n> The weight uses the discounted return relative to a baseline, not just the immediate reward. A positive return can be worse than the reference.\n\n> [!question]- Is the update guaranteed to make that action more probable?\n> The individual term supplies a local direction. The final update combines other steps, episodes, entropy and shared parameters, then uses Adam. A guaranteed probability increase for every positively weighted sample does not follow.\n\n> [!question]- Why not learn only from the final reward?\n> Reward-to-go includes all subsequent rewards, including intermediate feedback. It provides a learning signal for every sampled action. However, it cannot create a delivery signal that was never experienced.\n\n> [!question]- What should I memorise?\n> Memorise the flow: collect \u2192 return \u2192 scale \u2192 baseline \u2192 log-probability loss plus entropy \u2192 gradient update \u2192 baseline update. Understand each symbol well enough to reconstruct the equation rather than memorising its appearance.\n\n## Source trail\n\n- **Course explanation:** W10 pp. 13\u201324 and 28 in [[07_Sources_and_Presentation_Checklist|the course source map]].\n- **Policy-gradient and baseline derivations:** [[ReinforcementL/Materials/RLbook2020.pdf|Local textbook]], \u00a7\u00a713.1\u201313.5.\n- **Actual implementation:** [[final_report/reinforce_evidence/sources/MEDS_REINFORCE_v1_2_Tuning.ipynb|Archived notebook]], functions named above.\n- **Loss and settings:** [[final_report/2.4.3_REINFORCE|Implemented loss]], [[final_report/3.1.4_REINFORCE_Settings|Reported settings]].\n- **Observed results:** [[final_report/3.3.3_REINFORCE_Results|Results and limitations]].\n\n---\n\n[[03_Policy_Gradients|Back to algorithm comparison]] \u00b7 [[OralDefence/README|Study map]] \u00b7 [[05_Worked_Calculations|More practice]]\n"}];
const questions=[{"id": 0, "topic": 0, "prompt": "What does the Markov property require?", "options": ["The current state and action suffice for the next-state/reward distribution", "Every state is independent of every other state", "The agent must remember every past action", "Rewards must be deterministic"], "why": "History is unnecessary given a sufficient current state and action; this does not imply independence from the current state."}, {"id": 1, "topic": 0, "prompt": "Does a 17-feature observation establish full observability?", "options": ["No; distinct hidden states may share the same observation", "Yes; any fixed-length vector is a state", "Yes; neural networks recover all hidden information", "Only when the reward is positive"], "why": "MEDS hides some history and traffic information. The notes identify possible state aliasing, not a measured causal effect."}, {"id": 2, "topic": 0, "prompt": "With rewards 2, \u22121, 5 and \u03b3 = 0.9, what is G\u2080?", "options": ["5.15", "6", "3.5", "6.5"], "why": "G\u2080 = 2 + 0.9(\u22121) + 0.9\u00b2(5) = 5.15. Work backwards: 5 \u2192 3.5 \u2192 5.15."}, {"id": 3, "topic": 0, "prompt": "Q values are 4 and 8; policy probabilities are 0.25 and 0.75. What is V?", "options": ["7", "6", "8", "3"], "why": "V = 0.25 \u00d7 4 + 0.75 \u00d7 8 = 7. The average is weighted by the policy."}, {"id": 4, "topic": 0, "prompt": "If Q(s,a) = 4 and V(s) = 7, what is advantage?", "options": ["\u22123", "3", "11", "4/7"], "why": "A = Q \u2212 V = \u22123. A positive Q can still be below the policy average."}, {"id": 5, "topic": 0, "prompt": "Which continuation belongs in a Bellman optimality backup?", "options": ["The maximum next-action value", "Always the uniform average", "The minimum reward seen so far", "The most likely random transition only"], "why": "Optimality chooses the best action; stochastic transitions must still be probability-weighted."}, {"id": 6, "topic": 1, "prompt": "Which target is Monte Carlo?", "options": ["The complete sampled return G\u209c", "r + \u03b3V(s\u2032)", "r + \u03b3 max Q(s\u2032,a\u2032)", "A model-based average over next states"], "why": "MC waits for an observed episode return. Using an estimated continuation is bootstrapping."}, {"id": 7, "topic": 1, "prompt": "V = 4, r = 2, V(next) = 6, \u03b3 = 0.9, \u03b1 = 0.1. TD update?", "options": ["4.34", "7.4", "3.4", "4.6"], "why": "Target = 7.4; TD error = 3.4; updated V = 4 + 0.1 \u00d7 3.4 = 4.34."}, {"id": 8, "topic": 1, "prompt": "What distinguishes SARSA from Q-learning?", "options": ["SARSA uses the next action actually selected; Q-learning uses a max", "SARSA never samples", "Q-learning requires complete episodes", "SARSA is always model-based"], "why": "SARSA is on-policy. Q-learning targets a greedy continuation while behaviour may explore."}, {"id": 9, "topic": 1, "prompt": "What does bootstrapping mean?", "options": ["Using a current estimate within a learning target", "Drawing a random sample", "Training a neural network", "Resetting the environment"], "why": "TD bootstraps; MC samples without bootstrapping. These are separate properties."}, {"id": 10, "topic": 1, "prompt": "Why can a learner using a simulator still be model-free?", "options": ["It learns from samples without using a transition model for planning", "The simulator has no transition rules", "Model-free means no neural network", "Only real-world data can be model-free"], "why": "The distinction concerns how the learner uses dynamics, not whether a simulator exists."}, {"id": 11, "topic": 1, "prompt": "What is control in reinforcement learning?", "options": ["Improving the policy", "Estimating one fixed policy only", "Holding all actions constant", "Removing randomness from the simulator"], "why": "Prediction estimates values for a policy; control improves behaviour."}, {"id": 12, "topic": 2, "prompt": "Why is basic REINFORCE a Monte Carlo method?", "options": ["It weights policy gradients with sampled episode returns", "It uses a max over Q values", "It bootstraps from a critic each step", "It enumerates all state transitions"], "why": "The return is observed along a trajectory rather than replaced with an estimated continuation."}, {"id": 13, "topic": 2, "prompt": "What does an action-independent baseline do in the standard derivation?", "options": ["Its expected score contribution cancels", "It guarantees every update improves return", "It removes the need for exploration", "It makes all returns positive"], "why": "The action probabilities sum to one, whose derivative is zero. A useful baseline can reduce variance; an arbitrary baseline need not."}, {"id": 14, "topic": 2, "prompt": "What does PPO clip?", "options": ["The probability ratio in its surrogate objective", "All rewards to zero or one", "The observation dimension", "The episode length"], "why": "PPO ratio clipping differs from gradient-norm clipping and does not guarantee task success."}, {"id": 15, "topic": 2, "prompt": "For PPO r = 1.3, A = 2, \u03b5 = 0.2, what is the clipped surrogate term?", "options": ["2.4", "2.6", "1.2", "0.4"], "why": "min(rA, clip(r,0.8,1.2)A) = min(2.6,2.4) = 2.4."}, {"id": 16, "topic": 2, "prompt": "For PPO r = 0.7, A = \u22122, \u03b5 = 0.2, what is the surrogate term?", "options": ["\u22121.6", "\u22121.4", "1.6", "\u22122.4"], "why": "min(\u22121.4, 0.8 \u00d7 \u22122) = \u22121.6. The sign of advantage matters."}, {"id": 17, "topic": 3, "prompt": "What constitutes full MEDS success?", "options": ["All three deliveries, return to (0,0), then Stop", "Any pickup", "A positive total reward", "One delivery without a collision"], "why": "Success is a task-specific event. Pickups and mean delivery counts are separate outcomes."}, {"id": 18, "topic": 3, "prompt": "What did final sampled and greedy REINFORCE evaluations achieve?", "options": ["Zero pickups, deliveries and full successes in both 20-episode modes", "Greedy solved all deliveries", "Sampled achieved 53 pickups", "Both solved 20 episodes"], "why": "The 53 pickup events occurred in training across changing policies, not final evaluation."}, {"id": 19, "topic": 3, "prompt": "Can PPO and REINFORCE results establish an algorithm ranking here?", "options": ["No; traffic conditions and evidence differ", "Yes; 20/20 pickups proves PPO universally wins", "Yes; use reward only", "Yes; ignore checkpoint identity"], "why": "PPO pickup results used no traffic; REINFORCE final evaluation used traffic. A controlled comparison must match conditions and budgets."}, {"id": 20, "topic": 3, "prompt": "What does the 120-episode scripted-pickup diagnostic establish?", "options": ["Zero deliveries after assisted starts under those conditions", "Normal-reset success probability is exactly zero", "Pickup is the only cause of failure", "Every possible policy must fail"], "why": "It is conditional evidence, not a normal-reset evaluation or proof of a unique cause."}, {"id": 21, "topic": 3, "prompt": "How should bundled learning-rate and batch changes be interpreted?", "options": ["As one treatment unless components are compared separately", "Attribute gains to whichever change seems plausible", "Treat different training budgets as matched", "Call pickups full successes"], "why": "A combined treatment cannot identify each component\u2019s causal contribution."}, {"id": 22, "topic": 4, "prompt": "With V = 4, return = 10 and \u03b1 = 0.1, the MC update is\u2026", "options": ["4.6", "10", "6", "4.1"], "why": "V \u2190 4 + 0.1(10 \u2212 4) = 4.6."}, {"id": 23, "topic": 4, "prompt": "Reward is 2, next value is 5, \u03b3 = 0.9. One-step target?", "options": ["6.5", "7", "4.5", "2.9"], "why": "The target adds immediate reward to discounted continuation: 2 + 0.9 \u00d7 5."}, {"id": 24, "topic": 5, "prompt": "An examiner asks why MEDS failed. Which answer is defensible?", "options": ["Report observations, then label plausible causes as hypotheses", "Claim entropy definitely caused every collision", "Say negative loss proves exploration stopped", "Claim a single cause without an intervention"], "why": "The evidence shows failure and collision endings; it does not isolate causal mechanisms. Explain how you would test a hypothesis."}, {"id": 25, "topic": 5, "prompt": "What should accompany a result in a defence?", "options": ["Checkpoint, settings, denominator and outcome definition", "Only the highest reward", "Only an algorithm name", "An unlabelled percentage"], "why": "Provenance makes results interpretable; missing evidence should be acknowledged rather than guessed."}, {"id": 26, "topic": 6, "prompt": "What should you do with an unfinished presentation claim?", "options": ["Check the source and resolve or explicitly qualify it", "Invent a plausible number", "Use training results as evaluation", "Omit the denominator"], "why": "Use the source map and presentation checklist to identify evidence gaps before presenting."}, {"id": 27, "topic": 7, "prompt": "In the reported implementation, the EMA baseline is\u2026", "options": ["One moving-average scalar", "A learned value for every observation", "A Q-learning target network", "A probability of success"], "why": "The reported ema setting is not a state-dependent bootstrapping critic."}, {"id": 28, "topic": 7, "prompt": "G = 50, divisor = 100, baseline = 0.7. Return weight?", "options": ["\u22120.2", "0.2", "49.3", "50.7"], "why": "w = G/c \u2212 b = 0.5 \u2212 0.7 = \u22120.2. Positive absolute return can be worse than the reference."}, {"id": 29, "topic": 7, "prompt": "Old baseline = 0.3, batch mean = 0.5, decay = 0.9. New baseline?", "options": ["0.32", "0.48", "0.8", "0.2"], "why": "0.9 \u00d7 0.3 + 0.1 \u00d7 0.5 = 0.32. The code updates the baseline after the policy update."}, {"id": 30, "topic": 7, "prompt": "Does high action entropy guarantee successful exploration?", "options": ["No; diverse actions may still miss useful task sequences", "Yes; all states must be visited", "Yes; delivery is then guaranteed", "Only with eight actions"], "why": "Entropy measures spread over actions at an observation, not state coverage or successful pickup\u2013delivery sequences."}, {"id": 31, "topic": 7, "prompt": "Why is there a minus sign in the policy loss?", "options": ["The optimiser minimises while the return objective is maximised", "All returns must be negative", "Probabilities are negative", "The baseline is always negative"], "why": "Negating the ascent surrogate lets a minimisation optimiser follow the desired gradient direction."}, {"id": 32, "topic": 7, "prompt": "Can selected-action probability alone determine entropy?", "options": ["No; entropy depends on the full distribution", "Yes; H always equals \u2212log p(selected)", "Yes; H equals the return", "Only when the action succeeds"], "why": "Categorical entropy sums \u2212p log p over every action, not only the sampled one."}, {"id": 33, "topic": 7, "prompt": "What is the purpose of the outer \u03b3\u1d57 factor?", "options": ["Weight a step relative to the episode start", "Replace the learning rate", "Compute a success probability", "Make the baseline state-dependent"], "why": "Discount inside G is relative to the action time; the outer factor aligns weighting to the episode start."}];